WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.430 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:04.900 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:12.003 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.003 --> 00:00:13.670 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Today,
we're going to talk

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:16.770 align:middle line:84%
about peer-guided
learning at scale.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.637 align:middle line:84%
The thing I asked
you to do for today

00:00:18.637 --> 00:00:20.720 align:middle line:84%
was to write a little bit
in your learning journal

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.420 align:middle line:84%
about one of your
hobbies that you use

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.060 align:middle line:90%
online resources to learn from.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.510 align:middle line:84%
So just get in groups of three
and spend the first few minutes

00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:33.020 align:middle line:84%
talking to each other about
what kinds of hobbies you have

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.725 align:middle line:84%
and what kind of online learning
you do in those hobbies.

00:00:35.725 --> 00:00:36.600 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.010 align:middle line:90%
Ready?

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:37.510 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.070 align:middle line:90%


00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.650 align:middle line:90%
All right, come on back.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.020 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, Omar
should show us his camera.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you want to
talk about-- oh, yeah.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.760 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I talked
about photography.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.980 align:middle line:84%
I spend an unhealthy
amount of time on YouTube

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.430 align:middle line:90%
with photography tutorials.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.010 align:middle line:84%
And so this camera is
a 50-year-old camera,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.468 align:middle line:84%
and I had no idea how to
even begin looking for one.

00:00:59.468 --> 00:01:01.010 align:middle line:84%
And these last three
weeks, I've been

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:05.060 align:middle line:84%
looking because I want to
run a CPW event for admits

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.377 align:middle line:90%
on how to use an SLR camera.

00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:08.460 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Isn't that cool?

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.523 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: So I don't know--

00:01:10.523 --> 00:01:11.940 align:middle line:84%
this is in really
great condition.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.590 align:middle line:90%
And I was on eBay for hours.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.660 align:middle line:90%
But I watched YouTube tutorials.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.070 align:middle line:84%
I was reading on Reddit forums,
photography forums, DPReview,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.470 align:middle line:84%
PetaPixel on what
to look out for.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:25.432 align:middle line:84%
And before that, learning
how to actually take photos

00:01:25.432 --> 00:01:26.640 align:middle line:90%
was all done through YouTube.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.600 align:middle line:90%
It was self-taught.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.420 align:middle line:84%
I started off with
a phone, and then

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.870 align:middle line:84%
I slowly built up my toolbox
once I had the knowledge.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.800 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, now, I'm the
photo editor for The Tech,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.230 align:middle line:84%
I do stuff for
yearbook, and now I

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.647 align:middle line:84%
do a lot of stuff
for photography,

00:01:40.647 --> 00:01:42.440 align:middle line:84%
and now I'm actually
teaching others.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:43.700 align:middle line:84%
I recently actually
just interviewed--

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:44.742 align:middle line:90%
I forgot to mention this.

00:01:44.742 --> 00:01:47.580 align:middle line:84%
I interviewed to teach a
digital photography course,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.300 align:middle line:84%
although, due to time conflicts,
I won't be able to do that.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.650 align:middle line:84%
But I did interview, and I was
about to go on the other side

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.842 align:middle line:90%
of that learning experience.

00:01:57.842 --> 00:01:59.420 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: The camera's rad.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.050 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I just got it
before my last class.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.290 align:middle line:84%
So I need to go
to a camera shop,

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:07.880 align:middle line:84%
just to make sure
it works, which

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.039 align:middle line:90%
I'll be doing after this class.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:11.270 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.130 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I want
a photo of that.

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.630 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I
want a photo of Omar

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.356 align:middle line:84%
with his new camera taken
with a contemporary--

00:02:18.356 --> 00:02:20.360 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.510 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, I mean,
those Polaroid cameras

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.670 align:middle line:84%
became much more widely
accessible, probably when

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:26.820 align:middle line:90%
I was in grade school.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:27.990 align:middle line:90%
They were pretty common.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.750 align:middle line:84%
And then there was a revival
in the last few years.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.630 align:middle line:84%
So my daughter has
a Polaroid camera.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:35.270 align:middle line:84%
I don't think it's called--
we call them Polaroid cameras.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.160 align:middle line:84%
They probably have some
different term now,

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.290 align:middle line:84%
instant camera or
something like that.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.508 align:middle line:84%
Anybody have a completely
different hobby from Omar,

00:02:41.508 --> 00:02:43.550 align:middle line:84%
where you could tell a
very similar kind of story

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.200 align:middle line:90%
about learning?

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.130 align:middle line:90%


00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:50.870 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'm not an expert.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess a
similar story of learning

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.590 align:middle line:90%
is through YouTube, right?

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.230 align:middle line:84%
I want to-- or I'm
starting to learn

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:02.320 align:middle line:84%
how to play guitar
and bass and stuff.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.030 align:middle line:84%
But the thing with
YouTube is it's

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.610 align:middle line:84%
like, we talk about
algorithm-guided learning

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.290 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, but
it kind of almost

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.440 align:middle line:84%
feels like YouTube
is algorithm-guided

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.890 align:middle line:84%
learning in a way, or
I feel like the YouTube

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.530 align:middle line:84%
algorithm really
drives my interests

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.290 align:middle line:84%
or diverts my interests
into different things

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.580 align:middle line:90%
at different periods of time.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.160 align:middle line:84%
On a week-by-week-- on
a week-by-week basis,

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:28.990 align:middle line:84%
I can be interested in something
completely different than what

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.650 align:middle line:90%
I was interested in last week.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.220 align:middle line:84%
So it feels like I'm just
moving through a bunch

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.830 align:middle line:84%
of different hobbies
because I feel

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:38.380 align:middle line:84%
like I'm at the whim of the
YouTube algorithm sometimes.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.390 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh,
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:41.330 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:41.830 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:42.330 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:47.080 align:middle line:84%
No, I think people feel that way
with lots of technologies that

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.040 align:middle line:90%
do some kind of aggregation.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.680 align:middle line:84%
Pinterest for people who
do crafty kinds of things,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.530 align:middle line:84%
TikTok for people who follow
media, and stuff like that.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:55.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.130 align:middle line:84%
What other hobbies
do you talk about,

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:00.840 align:middle line:84%
or what other informal
learning strategies seem

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.180 align:middle line:84%
important and interesting
or things that you were

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.740 align:middle line:90%
talking about in your groups?

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:06.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Asa?

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'll say, on a
less, I guess, hands-on level,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.500 align:middle line:84%
I use similar resources, like
YouTube and stuff like that,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.490 align:middle line:84%
for a lot of collecting
knowledge for things that

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.890 align:middle line:84%
I'm interested in, like
fashion, for example,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:27.678 align:middle line:90%
because a lot of it is like--

00:04:27.678 --> 00:04:30.000 align:middle line:84%
in New York Fashion
Week, for example, it's

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.110 align:middle line:84%
these very condensed times
where it's all happening

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.800 align:middle line:84%
and a lot of people
follow very closely.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.925 align:middle line:84%
But if you're an MIT student,
you're probably like,

00:04:37.925 --> 00:04:39.910 align:middle line:90%
I can't be doing that.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:43.380 align:middle line:84%
But then you can watch
highlights from it

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.880 align:middle line:84%
and actual critics'
commentary on them

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:54.050 align:middle line:84%
and see what that's doing,
both socially and also

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.290 align:middle line:90%
from an artistic side.

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.082 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:04:56.082 --> 00:04:59.060 align:middle line:84%
Do you use Pinterest or
anything for collecting your--

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I had a
social media problem.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.120 align:middle line:90%
So now I just do YouTube.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.760 align:middle line:84%
So I'm kind of just continuing
into YouTube and stuff.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.300 align:middle line:84%
But I do get to, I guess,
reflect on it because I'm

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.012 align:middle line:90%
doing costume design and stuff.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:13.970 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, cool.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.250 align:middle line:84%
My daughter is big
into aesthetics

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.410 align:middle line:84%
of various kinds, craft
things or design things,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.573 align:middle line:84%
and Pinterest is a
big part of how she--

00:05:21.573 --> 00:05:23.490 align:middle line:84%
she does the same kind
of combing that you do,

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.850 align:middle line:84%
but she's deliberate about
taking things that she likes

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:28.490 align:middle line:84%
and storing them and
categorizing them and trying

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:31.280 align:middle line:84%
to make sense of them
and things like that.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:32.953 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Dana, then Imran.

00:05:32.953 --> 00:05:34.370 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was
just going to say,

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:39.240 align:middle line:84%
just another medium outside of
watching videos is just online.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:40.440 align:middle line:90%
So mine is cooking.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.520 align:middle line:90%
So recipes or just articles.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.340 align:middle line:84%
And then also, mine was
a little bit of TikTok,

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.060 align:middle line:84%
just in the one-minute
video of, you can cook this.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.800 align:middle line:84%
And it isn't a professional
chef showing you how to do it.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.650 align:middle line:84%
It was cool to see that, oh,
I can relate to that person,

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.710 align:middle line:90%
so I can do it.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.730 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: What are your
favorite recipe media or cooking

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:03.260 align:middle line:90%
media?

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:03.760 align:middle line:90%
How do you--

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I've only
perfected one singular dish,

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:11.260 align:middle line:84%
and it's scallops, which
is a relatively hard thing.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.830 align:middle line:84%
But I'm scared to cook a
chicken because I'm going

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:15.030 align:middle line:90%
to give myself salmonella.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.390 align:middle line:90%
But [LAUGHING] scallop.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.383 align:middle line:84%
So my family joke is, are you
only going to eat scallops

00:06:21.383 --> 00:06:22.467 align:middle line:90%
for the rest of your life?

00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:23.260 align:middle line:90%
I was like, yeah.

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:24.677 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH:
They're pretty good.

00:06:24.677 --> 00:06:25.890 align:middle line:90%
They're healthy.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:27.390 align:middle line:84%
There might be too
many heavy metals

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.640 align:middle line:84%
if you do that your whole
life or something like that.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:30.270 align:middle line:90%
But yeah.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:31.770 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I don't know
if bottom feeders

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:33.880 align:middle line:84%
are what you should do
for your whole life,

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.150 align:middle line:84%
but that seems like
seems like a good start.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.830 align:middle line:90%
How about you, Imran?

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I didn't
mention this in our group,

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.400 align:middle line:84%
but what came out
from people talking,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.930 align:middle line:84%
I feel like a lot of people
relate, is Numberphile.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:47.590 align:middle line:90%


00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:48.790 align:middle line:90%
3Blue1Brown, this video.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:49.585 align:middle line:90%
I just put them in.

00:06:49.585 --> 00:06:50.710 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: What is this?

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.230 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's a math video.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.177 align:middle line:84%
They just tell you
cool math stuff.

00:06:55.177 --> 00:06:56.760 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It's
called Numberphile?

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:57.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Numberphile.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:57.960 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:58.880 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 3Blue1Brown.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.380 align:middle line:90%
Those kind of videos.

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:04.010 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes you learn that
there is fractional dimension,

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:07.610 align:middle line:84%
and you'd just be
watching what that means.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I watch
those too sometimes.

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.678 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They're
very interesting.

00:07:11.678 --> 00:07:12.470 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.428 align:middle line:84%
Who was the guy who came
here a couple of years

00:07:14.428 --> 00:07:17.015 align:middle line:84%
ago for a graduation or
convocation or something?

00:07:17.015 --> 00:07:17.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Mark Rober?

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:18.350 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Hm?

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:19.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Mark Rober.

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:19.680 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Mark Rober.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.030 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:20.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:20.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:21.870 align:middle line:90%
He's a science YouTuber.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.590 align:middle line:84%
A guy named Veritasium
does things like that, too.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:26.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:26.590 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:31.700 align:middle line:84%
Enormously popular and
successful and things like that.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:35.287 align:middle line:84%
I play a lot of computer
games, and my life overlaps.

00:07:35.287 --> 00:07:37.370 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, computer
games are a couple of years

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:38.790 align:middle line:90%
older than me, but not by much.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.748 align:middle line:84%
I mean, definitely some
of my earliest memories

00:07:40.748 --> 00:07:44.400 align:middle line:84%
are games that would have been
played off of a cassette reel,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.260 align:middle line:84%
rather than any kind of rotary
disk or something like that.

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:50.620 align:middle line:84%
But another
distinctive memory is

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:52.690 align:middle line:84%
that one of the first
categories of games

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.910 align:middle line:84%
were these text-based
games, where

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:58.610 align:middle line:84%
you had to enter prompts into
a parser, like open mailbox,

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:02.900 align:middle line:84%
go left, go right, walk in,
pick up key, put key in lock,

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:03.860 align:middle line:90%
those kinds of things.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.943 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of what you're
doing-- yeah, come on in--

00:08:05.943 --> 00:08:10.510 align:middle line:84%
was kind of guessing what
was the right key term

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:13.125 align:middle line:90%
to put in to engage things.

00:08:13.125 --> 00:08:14.750 align:middle line:84%
And so actually, a
lot of these games--

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.020 align:middle line:84%
Infocom was a company
that sold a lot of them--

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:19.280 align:middle line:84%
the game companies would
also publish a book.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:21.910 align:middle line:84%
And the book would
have the answers

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:25.070 align:middle line:84%
to a whole bunch of things or
solution sets in invisible ink.

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.805 align:middle line:84%
And so you had a little
pen that came with it.

00:08:27.805 --> 00:08:29.680 align:middle line:84%
And so when you decided
that you had given up

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.460 align:middle line:84%
trying for yourself, you
would cover the invisible ink

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.830 align:middle line:84%
with this pen that would
reveal the next clue

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:39.900 align:middle line:90%
and help you go on and so forth.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:44.169 align:middle line:84%
But now, essentially every video
game, they almost instantly

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:48.890 align:middle line:84%
are created with a wiki that
has every item in the game

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.230 align:middle line:84%
and what they do
and where they go.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:54.440 align:middle line:90%
A Reddit forum for conversation.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.100 align:middle line:84%
More recently,
Discord pages that

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:01.250 align:middle line:84%
actually, for smaller games,
can be kind of surprisingly

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:02.870 align:middle line:84%
intimate, where
you really do feel

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:04.828 align:middle line:84%
like you're just like
chatting with the guy who

00:09:04.828 --> 00:09:07.310 align:middle line:84%
made the game, even
if they may not

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:08.640 align:middle line:90%
be responding to you directly.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.000 align:middle line:84%
But they're certainly responding
to people that are there around

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.250 align:middle line:90%
with you and things like that.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:14.600 align:middle line:84%
YouTube channels or all
these kinds of things.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.070 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I think there's
all kinds of ways.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:20.990 align:middle line:84%
There are very few
people, I think, left

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:26.510 align:middle line:84%
in the networked world who don't
have some kind of encounter

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.850 align:middle line:84%
with this sort of
informal learning.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:33.560 align:middle line:84%
I think just an enormous number
of people in a very short period

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:34.080 align:middle line:90%
of time--

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.580 align:middle line:84%
I mean, obviously,
there have always

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.100 align:middle line:84%
been people who do DIY kinds
of things and stuff like that,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.800 align:middle line:84%
but the degree to which online
resources has shaped those

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.630 align:middle line:84%
learning interactions
is a profound change

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:49.220 align:middle line:90%
over the last decade or two.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:54.170 align:middle line:84%
And I think it reminds us to do
a few things that are useful.

00:09:54.170 --> 00:10:00.580 align:middle line:84%
One is to conceptualize
learning really broadly.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:02.330 align:middle line:84%
A lot of times, when
we think of learning,

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:06.077 align:middle line:84%
we pin it down in
education, K-12 education,

00:10:06.077 --> 00:10:07.160 align:middle line:90%
college, things like that.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:10.600 align:middle line:84%
But that's a relatively
small part of people's lives.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.770 align:middle line:84%
People are learning all
throughout their lives,

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:13.990 align:middle line:90%
all the time.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:16.172 align:middle line:84%
People are developing
new skills for work.

00:10:16.172 --> 00:10:18.380 align:middle line:84%
They're developing new skills
around their interests.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.943 align:middle line:90%
They're pursuing passions.

00:10:19.943 --> 00:10:21.110 align:middle line:90%
They're doing whatever else.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:22.570 align:middle line:84%
And so I think
it's very helpful,

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:24.130 align:middle line:84%
when thinking about
online learning,

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:27.800 align:middle line:84%
to be really open to all
these kinds of things.

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:30.970 align:middle line:84%
And then I would say, if
there's a core argument

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.530 align:middle line:84%
in peer-guided
learning at scale,

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.010 align:middle line:84%
it's that these
technologies have

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:39.490 align:middle line:84%
been so profound in
changing the ways

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:42.080 align:middle line:84%
that we pursue learning
outside of school,

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:44.940 align:middle line:84%
and schools have proven
to be institutions

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:48.600 align:middle line:84%
that are just remarkably
resistant to these forms

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:49.840 align:middle line:90%
of learning as well.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.860 align:middle line:84%
My hunch is most of you don't
have an experience where

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.050 align:middle line:84%
you feel like, yeah,
the kind of learning

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:57.300 align:middle line:84%
that I did in my eighth
grade physics class

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.010 align:middle line:84%
was really just like
the kind of learning

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.950 align:middle line:84%
that I did to learn photography
or something like that.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:05.940 align:middle line:84%
There's something about schools
and the design and organization

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:08.700 align:middle line:84%
of schools that makes it really
hard for these kinds of learning

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.550 align:middle line:84%
environments to
make their way in.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.400 align:middle line:84%
I think a lot of what I
want to share with you today

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.960 align:middle line:84%
are some of the most
substantial efforts,

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.180 align:middle line:84%
particularly in
higher education,

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:21.750 align:middle line:84%
to figure out how some
of these approaches

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:26.850 align:middle line:84%
to peer-guided learning at
scale could make their way

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:30.150 align:middle line:90%
into formal institutions.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:33.930 align:middle line:84%
The ideas that I am presenting
to you, if I was talking about

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:37.800 align:middle line:84%
them 10 years ago, I would
have positioned them more as,

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:43.300 align:middle line:84%
these are very viable ways in
which at least higher education,

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.110 align:middle line:84%
maybe a number of
forms of education,

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:48.680 align:middle line:84%
might substantially change
in the next 5 or 10 years.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:52.700 align:middle line:84%
And I think I'm more likely
to present you them today

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.350 align:middle line:84%
as an interesting dead
end, as a set of things

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.350 align:middle line:84%
that people were trying,
putting a lot of effort

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:59.090 align:middle line:84%
behind, putting a
lot of design behind,

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:04.100 align:middle line:84%
and really didn't have
much impact in how

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.800 align:middle line:84%
especially higher
education, in how

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.370 align:middle line:84%
formal education of all
kinds are organized.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:13.418 align:middle line:84%
Studying things
that flop is good.

00:12:13.418 --> 00:12:15.710 align:middle line:84%
We're going to come back to
this a few different times.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:17.585 align:middle line:84%
Studying things that
flop is really important

00:12:17.585 --> 00:12:19.560 align:middle line:90%
to figure out why things work.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:21.667 align:middle line:84%
If you just study
things that work,

00:12:21.667 --> 00:12:23.250 align:middle line:84%
you actually don't
know why they work.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:24.708 align:middle line:84%
You know characteristics
they have,

00:12:24.708 --> 00:12:26.210 align:middle line:84%
but there's lots
of things that are

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:28.793 align:middle line:84%
in common between things that
work and things that don't work.

00:12:28.793 --> 00:12:33.925 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what we'll
delve into some today.

00:12:33.925 --> 00:12:35.300 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll talk
for a little bit,

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.540 align:middle line:84%
and then see what kind
of thoughts you have.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:37.040 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:38.850 align:middle line:84%
So I think we did the
Rainbow Loom story.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.440 align:middle line:84%
We talked about
this the other day.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:43.080 align:middle line:84%
This is one, I
think, iconic moment

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:48.030 align:middle line:84%
of the creation of a
new craft generates

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.750 align:middle line:90%
these new kinds of communities.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:53.910 align:middle line:90%
This was from 2013.

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:55.450 align:middle line:84%
"Low tech but high
on creativity,

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:56.950 align:middle line:84%
the Rainbow Loom
has kids hooked--

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:59.740 align:middle line:84%
spending hours after school and
on weekends making bracelets

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:03.330 align:middle line:84%
weaving together small, colorful
rubber bands on a simple plastic

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.170 align:middle line:90%
loom."

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:10.480 align:middle line:84%
And all of the narrative around
Rainbow Loom was, man, look,

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.070 align:middle line:84%
we finally got kids
off their phones.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:15.570 align:middle line:84%
We finally got kids in the real
world connecting to one another.

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:18.300 align:middle line:84%
This is, I think, 2013, that
this was published right

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:19.860 align:middle line:90%
before Christmas.

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:23.627 align:middle line:84%
But of course, the kids
were on their phones

00:13:23.627 --> 00:13:25.960 align:middle line:84%
the whole time that they were
playing with Rainbow Loom.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:28.320 align:middle line:84%
And so this is
Ashley and Steph who

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:30.878 align:middle line:84%
were these two girls
who have a quarter

00:13:30.878 --> 00:13:32.170 align:middle line:90%
of a million people subscribed.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:34.878 align:middle line:84%
Oh, this is, yeah, how to make a
Rainbow Loom Starburst bracelet.

00:13:34.878 --> 00:13:37.680 align:middle line:84%
Whenever I recorded
this, 30 million views,

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.400 align:middle line:84%
probably 10 times as many that
were on the official Rainbow

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:44.030 align:middle line:84%
Loom Starburst page,
that somehow these two

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:46.528 align:middle line:90%
kids had captivated--

00:13:46.528 --> 00:13:47.070 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:48.570 align:middle line:84%
It's hard to say that it
was millions of people,

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.862 align:middle line:84%
because it actually could
have been a couple of hundred

00:13:50.862 --> 00:13:53.150 align:middle line:84%
thousand people each
watched this 10 or 20 times

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.970 align:middle line:84%
because it's super hard to
make one of these bracelets.

00:13:55.970 --> 00:14:05.390 align:middle line:84%
But I think a compelling example
of how network learning was.

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:08.780 align:middle line:84%
And something like
this, if you're my age,

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:12.050 align:middle line:84%
looking at this in
2013, it's really hard

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:17.030 align:middle line:84%
to imagine something
similar happening in 1990

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:18.350 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:14:18.350 --> 00:14:20.430 align:middle line:84%
If someone had invented
a new kind of craft,

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.640 align:middle line:84%
you would have had to
go to a craft store,

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.140 align:middle line:84%
you would have had to join a
club, you had to go to classes,

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:28.160 align:middle line:84%
there would have to be a college
club or something like that.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:32.570 align:middle line:84%
I mean, maybe PBS would put
on a television show about it.

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:35.300 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine the Bob Ross
of Rainbow Loom or something

00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:36.440 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:42.400 align:middle line:84%
But very hard to imagine
the same kind of highly

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:47.410 align:middle line:84%
decentralized mechanism
by which learners

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.190 align:middle line:84%
connect themselves
to each other.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:50.648 align:middle line:84%
I think one of the
things I'm going

00:14:50.648 --> 00:14:52.790 align:middle line:84%
to ask you to read
for an upcoming week--

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:54.940 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember
if it's next week,

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:59.380 align:middle line:84%
but this piece by Ivan Illich
called "Deschooling Society."

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.900 align:middle line:84%
I think it's written
in the 1960s.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:04.300 align:middle line:90%
And he almost exactly--

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:06.410 align:middle line:90%
the very dawn of computing.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:11.020 align:middle line:84%
And he predicts learning
networks that emerge like this.

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:12.347 align:middle line:90%
He says, one day--

00:15:12.347 --> 00:15:14.180 align:middle line:84%
I mean, he has a funny
way of describing it.

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:17.147 align:middle line:84%
He says, one day, there's
going to be a bunch of learners

00:15:17.147 --> 00:15:19.480 align:middle line:84%
who write down the things
they're interested in learning

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:22.105 align:middle line:84%
on punch cards, because that's
what were computers at the time,

00:15:22.105 --> 00:15:24.280 align:middle line:84%
and there's going to be a
bunch of experts who share

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:25.790 align:middle line:90%
their interests on punch cards.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:27.100 align:middle line:84%
And the computers are going
to connect these punch

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.582 align:middle line:84%
cards together and get
people teaching and learning

00:15:29.582 --> 00:15:30.290 align:middle line:90%
from one another.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:33.080 align:middle line:84%
And it turned out, we had
YouTube rather than punch cards,

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:35.876 align:middle line:84%
but it was a pretty
similar kind of phenomenon.

00:15:35.876 --> 00:15:41.000 align:middle line:90%


00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.780 align:middle line:84%
In the early 2010s, there
were some people who were

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:46.962 align:middle line:84%
particularly enthusiastic
about the fact--

00:15:46.962 --> 00:15:48.920 align:middle line:84%
so one thing about Rainbow
Loom, you could say,

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.500 align:middle line:84%
is something like, well, all
right, so some kids figured out

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:52.920 align:middle line:90%
how to make plastic bracelets.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:54.290 align:middle line:90%
That's OK.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:57.650 align:middle line:84%
But there are
people who achieved

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:01.400 align:middle line:84%
elite levels of performance in a
variety of different disciplines

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:05.630 align:middle line:84%
and domains by following
the same kinds of practices

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:07.950 align:middle line:84%
that the Rainbow
Loom kids were using.

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:10.680 align:middle line:84%
So there's a guy named
Julius Yego who, as I recall,

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:12.690 align:middle line:84%
won a silver medal
in the Olympics.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.270 align:middle line:84%
He was from Kenya, and
he was a javelin thrower.

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:17.520 align:middle line:84%
There are no field
athletes in Kenya.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:19.130 align:middle line:90%
They're all track runners.

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:22.440 align:middle line:84%
And for whatever reason,
Julius Yego got it in his head,

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:25.550 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be a
javelin thrower.

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:27.140 align:middle line:90%
I mean, it's here.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:29.360 align:middle line:84%
"My coach is me, and
the YouTube videos."

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:32.330 align:middle line:84%
And he became an elite,
globally recognized--

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:34.470 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there's a
moment where this tips.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:37.240 align:middle line:84%
There's a moment where he
goes to a track and field

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:38.638 align:middle line:90%
competition.

00:16:38.638 --> 00:16:40.930 align:middle line:84%
He doesn't really have a
coach, and he throws a javelin

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:41.450 align:middle line:90%
pretty far.

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:42.850 align:middle line:84%
And some people notice
him and take him

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:44.308 align:middle line:84%
under his wing and
stuff like that.

00:16:44.308 --> 00:16:47.500 align:middle line:84%
So it's not just YouTube
videos that get you entirely

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.830 align:middle line:84%
to being the second best
javelin thrower in the world,

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:53.090 align:middle line:90%
but going quite a long way.

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:56.110 align:middle line:84%
There's echoes of this, of the
story that I told you before

00:16:56.110 --> 00:17:00.410 align:middle line:84%
of Battushig, the boy
genius of Ulaanbaatar,

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:03.762 align:middle line:84%
the kid who discovered
in Mongolia, through MITx

00:17:03.762 --> 00:17:05.470 align:middle line:84%
and things like this,
the idea that these

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.619 align:middle line:84%
could have these profound
democratizing effects.

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:12.310 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to
be in a country that

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:15.579 align:middle line:84%
has a really big track
and field infrastructure

00:17:15.579 --> 00:17:17.390 align:middle line:84%
to be able to become
a great athlete.

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:21.040 align:middle line:84%
You can do it anywhere
because we have YouTube now.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:28.810 align:middle line:84%
So I was working for
HarvardX in 2013.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:33.010 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll pause to
tell this story too.

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:35.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, I don't know what
the right order is now

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:36.100 align:middle line:90%
that I'm inspired by it.

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:40.000 align:middle line:84%
But there was this term,
massive open online courses,

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:43.050 align:middle line:84%
and it actually was
not originally applied

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:47.820 align:middle line:84%
to the courses that came out
of Coursera and edX and MITx

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:49.330 align:middle line:84%
and Udacity and
things like that.

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:51.540 align:middle line:84%
There were a group
of Canadian educators

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:56.400 align:middle line:84%
who, 2008, 2009, 2010,
2011, started building

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:59.220 align:middle line:90%
these networked courses.

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:01.810 align:middle line:84%
I'll go more into the details
of the network courses,

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:05.170 align:middle line:84%
but you would basically create
an online learning environment,

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:08.920 align:middle line:84%
have people create blogs,
connect on Twitter,

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:11.200 align:middle line:84%
form social network
connections with one another,

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:13.883 align:middle line:84%
and all be studying the
same thing at the same time.

00:18:13.883 --> 00:18:16.050 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes some of the people
would be getting credit

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:17.700 align:middle line:84%
from some institution
and other people

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:18.867 align:middle line:90%
would not be getting credit.

00:18:18.867 --> 00:18:21.150 align:middle line:84%
They were all kind of
openly being involved.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:23.460 align:middle line:84%
A few thousand people,
probably the largest

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.390 align:middle line:90%
of these, signing up.

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:26.910 align:middle line:84%
And so there was
actually a reporter

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:29.650 align:middle line:84%
who, when edX and
Coursera got started,

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:32.630 align:middle line:84%
was trying to figure out what
these things should be called.

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:34.910 align:middle line:84%
And somebody said, well,
these Canadian guys

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:37.380 align:middle line:84%
have these things called
massive open online courses.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:40.310 align:middle line:84%
And the name stuck, but it stuck
to describe something very, very

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:41.510 align:middle line:90%
different.

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:45.020 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that
we recognize early on

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:47.690 align:middle line:84%
is that, in the
early days of people

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:50.390 align:middle line:84%
doing the massive open
online courses that came out

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:54.590 align:middle line:84%
of Harvard and MIT, is many
of them were not just using

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:56.750 align:middle line:84%
the course materials
that were being provided

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:58.550 align:middle line:84%
on these platforms,
but they were using

00:18:58.550 --> 00:18:59.820 align:middle line:90%
all kinds of other things.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:02.940 align:middle line:84%
So you'd ask people and
they're like, yeah, well,

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:05.280 align:middle line:84%
we're searching for all
kinds of things that show up.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:07.310 align:middle line:84%
We're reading the
Wikipedia entries

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:09.330 align:middle line:84%
about the things that
show up in these classes.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:12.110 align:middle line:84%
We're buying additional
books that are around them.

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:14.250 align:middle line:84%
We're looking for those
books on Google Books.

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:16.310 align:middle line:84%
We're joining Facebook
groups of people

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:19.400 align:middle line:84%
who are studying these courses
together and things like that.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:25.070 align:middle line:84%
And so the barriers between
these instructor-guided learning

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:27.620 align:middle line:84%
at scale, these highly
structured learning management

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:31.040 align:middle line:84%
system kinds of things,
and the informal world

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:35.010 align:middle line:84%
of people messing around on
YouTube, learning about stuff,

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:35.950 align:middle line:90%
smushed together.

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:38.310 align:middle line:84%
Probably a bunch of you
have some kind of experience

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:40.240 align:middle line:90%
of that in your classes too.

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:42.323 align:middle line:84%
I mean, presumably
all of you, when

00:19:42.323 --> 00:19:43.990 align:middle line:84%
you don't understand
something in class,

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:45.698 align:middle line:84%
you're looking up
online videos about it.

00:19:45.698 --> 00:19:47.835 align:middle line:84%
Or you don't like the
lecture and, probably

00:19:47.835 --> 00:19:49.210 align:middle line:84%
mostly in your
technical classes,

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:50.668 align:middle line:84%
you watch it from
some other place.

00:19:50.668 --> 00:19:52.530 align:middle line:84%
Or I don't know, do
any of you join groups

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:55.140 align:middle line:84%
or find communities
of people who

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.752 align:middle line:84%
are good at solving
problems in the things

00:19:57.752 --> 00:19:58.835 align:middle line:90%
that you're interested in?

00:19:58.835 --> 00:20:01.353 align:middle line:90%


00:20:01.353 --> 00:20:03.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: One thing about
being an MIT student

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:05.470 align:middle line:84%
is you can go on OCW
if you miss a lecture

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:06.970 align:middle line:84%
or you don't
understand the lecture,

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:08.220 align:middle line:84%
you want to expand
it in a different way

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:09.520 align:middle line:90%
from a different professor.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:11.550 align:middle line:84%
You can just watch
that same lecture,

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:14.320 align:middle line:84%
which I've done before for some
of my more technical classes.

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:14.640 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:15.190 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.260 align:middle line:90%
So you can rewatch past things.

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:19.230 align:middle line:84%
Some professors are pretty
explicit about that.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:20.980 align:middle line:84%
They'll be like, well,
this is my lecture,

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:24.250 align:middle line:84%
but somebody else filmed roughly
the same course four years ago.

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:25.973 align:middle line:84%
And you can watch
all those things.

00:20:25.973 --> 00:20:27.390 align:middle line:84%
How many of your
classes still use

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:31.490 align:middle line:84%
Piazza or some kind of
discussion forum things?

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:34.080 align:middle line:84%
Are the Piazza forums
ever insufficient,

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:36.140 align:middle line:84%
and then you go
to Stack Overflow

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:38.885 align:middle line:84%
or to some other kind of--
maybe for your coding classes

00:20:38.885 --> 00:20:39.760 align:middle line:90%
and things like that?

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:44.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I would say, Piazza
has a weird social dynamic

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:45.990 align:middle line:90%
in itself.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:48.200 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Tell me more.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:50.330 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I think it's like--

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:53.660 align:middle line:84%
I mean, obviously you can
both do anonymous questions

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:54.990 align:middle line:90%
and non-anonymous questions.

00:20:54.990 --> 00:21:01.250 align:middle line:84%
But usually it seems like
it really depends on--

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:04.580 align:middle line:84%
a little bit on who's
really using it,

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:09.800 align:middle line:84%
and also, how responsive
the faculty and stuff are

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:12.600 align:middle line:90%
and how they choose to respond.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:18.080 align:middle line:84%
Because I have it in quite a
few classes where I log on,

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:21.350 align:middle line:84%
and then it's like, yes,
I had that question.

00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:24.020 align:middle line:84%
And they're like, oh, sorry,
we worded the question weird.

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:26.480 align:middle line:84%
But then I've also been in
quite a few classes where

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:30.580 align:middle line:84%
it's like, they're asking
this super high-level question

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:32.630 align:middle line:90%
about the lecture.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:36.560 align:middle line:84%
And then it kind of sets that
expectation for the class,

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:38.990 align:middle line:84%
especially if it's a
larger class, of, oh,

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:42.610 align:middle line:84%
my, quote unquote, "dumb"
questions aren't going to be

00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:43.430 align:middle line:90%
answered here.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:45.190 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: It's not going
to be respected or things

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:45.650 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:46.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:49.760 align:middle line:84%
And I mean, because these
things are social in nature,

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:51.610 align:middle line:84%
I bet a lot of
those things can be

00:21:51.610 --> 00:21:54.460 align:middle line:84%
determined pretty
early on by the actions

00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:56.110 align:middle line:90%
of a couple of people.

00:21:56.110 --> 00:21:58.570 align:middle line:84%
When we were studying massive
open online courses, one

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:00.850 align:middle line:84%
of the things we found
in the discussion forums,

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:04.160 align:middle line:84%
the largest threads, the
most upvoted kinds of things,

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:08.450 align:middle line:84%
were almost always amongst
the very first topics posted.

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:12.130 align:middle line:84%
So it wasn't like the
forums were surfacing--

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:14.510 align:middle line:84%
it wasn't like there
was lots of ideas,

00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:16.750 align:middle line:84%
and the most interesting
or important of those ideas

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:17.833 align:middle line:90%
were surfacing at the top.

00:22:17.833 --> 00:22:20.210 align:middle line:84%
It was like somebody
jumped in there first,

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:23.080 align:middle line:84%
and then people started
talking about those things.

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:25.900 align:middle line:84%
And you could imagine
an alternate timeline

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.290 align:middle line:84%
of the exact same course you
were in, where the first two

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.150 align:middle line:84%
people to post
happened to just be

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:31.320 align:middle line:84%
people who were like,
I don't understand

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:32.140 align:middle line:90%
this really basic thing.

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:33.390 align:middle line:90%
Will someone explain it again?

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:37.300 align:middle line:84%
And the forums have a whole
different character to them.

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.810 align:middle line:84%
I mean, to some extent, I
think of computer programming

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:42.270 align:middle line:84%
in particular as a
field that depends

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:44.610 align:middle line:84%
upon this kind of
informal learning,

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:48.072 align:middle line:84%
that basically no one knows
enough about programming

00:22:48.072 --> 00:22:50.530 align:middle line:84%
languages and how they operate
and how they solve problems.

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:53.460 align:middle line:84%
And so you essentially
can't do that profession

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:55.452 align:middle line:84%
if you can't figure
out how to navigate

00:22:55.452 --> 00:22:57.660 align:middle line:84%
all the online resources,
all the online communities,

00:22:57.660 --> 00:22:58.535 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:22:58.535 --> 00:23:02.970 align:middle line:84%
And unusually, you
can be promoted

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:07.170 align:middle line:84%
and thrive in those communities
through your participation

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.400 align:middle line:84%
in some of these kind of
informal learning networks.

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:13.290 align:middle line:84%
You can be really active in
creating open source projects

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:15.983 align:middle line:84%
or just be a really active
contributor to Stack Overflow

00:23:15.983 --> 00:23:17.650 align:middle line:84%
or other kinds of
communities like that,

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:19.350 align:middle line:84%
get recognized for
your contributions,

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:21.910 align:middle line:84%
and have that translate into
other kinds of opportunities.

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:25.240 align:middle line:90%
So the boundaries between--

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:27.473 align:middle line:84%
there are ways that I think
these informal learning

00:23:27.473 --> 00:23:29.390 align:middle line:84%
environments and formal
learning environments,

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:31.150 align:middle line:84%
the boundaries
can permeate, even

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:33.490 align:middle line:84%
if, as we'll keep talking
about, just trying

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:35.772 align:middle line:84%
to take the whole informal,
peer-guided learning

00:23:35.772 --> 00:23:37.480 align:middle line:84%
infrastructure and
bringing it to schools

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.645 align:middle line:90%
seems not to work pretty well.

00:23:39.645 --> 00:23:42.160 align:middle line:90%


00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:46.840 align:middle line:84%
I think, actually, one of
the most compelling reasons

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:50.620 align:middle line:84%
for integrating technology into
people's school learning at all

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:54.500 align:middle line:84%
is the idea that, actually,
for most of your lives,

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:57.370 align:middle line:84%
the kind of learning that
you do will look more

00:23:57.370 --> 00:23:59.020 align:middle line:84%
like the learning
around your hobbies

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:01.510 align:middle line:84%
than it does look like the
learning in your classes.

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:03.430 align:middle line:84%
In the next couple
of years, some of you

00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:06.055 align:middle line:84%
will keep taking classes for a
long, long time, but most of you

00:24:06.055 --> 00:24:08.510 align:middle line:84%
will probably stop, or take
them only infrequently.

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:11.380 align:middle line:84%
You will probably,
however, continue

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:13.945 align:middle line:84%
to participate in these
online learning communities.

00:24:13.945 --> 00:24:15.820 align:middle line:84%
To me, it raises really
interesting questions

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:19.840 align:middle line:84%
like, what responsibility
does a school have

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:24.810 align:middle line:84%
for helping people become
really good at learning cooking

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:27.130 align:middle line:84%
through playing with recipes
the way Dana described,

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:30.870 align:middle line:84%
or learning photography
through online forums, or music

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:33.150 align:middle line:84%
through online
videos, in the way

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:35.340 align:middle line:90%
that Jaren and Omar describe?

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:39.780 align:middle line:84%
What would a K-12 education
look like if someone left school

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:42.030 align:middle line:84%
and they were like,
I'm totally prepared

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:43.568 align:middle line:84%
to teach myself
stuff by engaging

00:24:43.568 --> 00:24:46.110 align:middle line:84%
these online communities because
I've had a bunch of practice

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:47.350 align:middle line:90%
and those sorts of things?

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.920 align:middle line:84%
What would a college
education look

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:51.100 align:middle line:90%
like if it was good at that?

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:53.600 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like that's
like such an interesting point,

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:56.820 align:middle line:84%
because I think back to a
conversation I had with my mom,

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:00.840 align:middle line:84%
where she grew up and was out of
college-- or out of high school

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:04.530 align:middle line:84%
right before the really dawn of
computer classes in high school

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:05.340 align:middle line:90%
and middle school.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:09.070 align:middle line:84%
And in middle school,
so rudimentary to us,

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:10.770 align:middle line:84%
we learn how to
use an Excel sheet

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:14.370 align:middle line:84%
and just learn how to Google
things and a lot of just

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:18.750 align:middle line:84%
the super basic skills, like how
to type on a computer keyboard

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:20.230 align:middle line:90%
that she doesn't know how to do.

00:25:20.230 --> 00:25:22.080 align:middle line:84%
And it almost seems
like the next generation

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:24.560 align:middle line:84%
will leave computer
classes knowing

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:25.920 align:middle line:90%
how to navigate these sites.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:29.450 align:middle line:84%
And then all the stuff that
I had to learn on my own,

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:32.130 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'm asking my kids,
where did you learn that?

00:25:32.130 --> 00:25:33.998 align:middle line:84%
And they're just
like, in school.

00:25:33.998 --> 00:25:35.540 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: There
was a phrase that

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:37.050 align:middle line:90%
was used for a number of years.

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:39.500 align:middle line:90%
It was called "digital native."

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:42.920 align:middle line:84%
Maybe a more common phrase that
you'll hear probably in the next

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:44.840 align:middle line:84%
few years will be
something like "AI native."

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:46.340 align:middle line:84%
The idea that,
what's the difference

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:48.440 align:middle line:84%
between having to
learn something

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:53.300 align:middle line:84%
by having it come into your life
versus having it be something

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:55.070 align:middle line:84%
that you grow up
with all the time.

00:25:55.070 --> 00:25:56.820 align:middle line:84%
There's actually a
bunch of good evidence,

00:25:56.820 --> 00:25:59.060 align:middle line:84%
in fact, that digital natives,
that young people are actually

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:01.602 align:middle line:84%
not that much better at using
technology, especially learning

00:26:01.602 --> 00:26:03.590 align:middle line:90%
things, than older folks.

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:05.090 align:middle line:84%
Young people in
schools are usually

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:06.727 align:middle line:84%
pretty good at
doing certain kinds

00:26:06.727 --> 00:26:08.810 align:middle line:84%
of technology-mediated
things, but the things that

00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:10.643 align:middle line:84%
are most helpful for
learning, they're often

00:26:10.643 --> 00:26:12.920 align:middle line:90%
no better at than anybody else.

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:14.880 align:middle line:84%
Although I've always
liked the idea--

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:16.880 align:middle line:84%
one of the things that
comes along with the idea

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:18.873 align:middle line:84%
of immigration of
digital natives--

00:26:18.873 --> 00:26:20.540 align:middle line:84%
digital immigrants
and digital natives--

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:24.218 align:middle line:84%
is that second-generation
immigrants are always

00:26:24.218 --> 00:26:26.010 align:middle line:84%
taking on new
responsibilities for teaching

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.090 align:middle line:84%
their first-generation
parents about things.

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:30.967 align:middle line:84%
You grow up in a country
and you learn the language,

00:26:30.967 --> 00:26:33.300 align:middle line:84%
you learn their culture, you
learn other kinds of things

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.008 align:middle line:84%
that you have to
explain to your parents.

00:26:35.008 --> 00:26:37.230 align:middle line:84%
So there's something kind
of nice about that metaphor

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:38.145 align:middle line:90%
carrying over.

00:26:38.145 --> 00:26:39.270 align:middle line:90%
Were you going to say more?

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:41.687 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Something that I
know the state of Massachusetts

00:26:41.687 --> 00:26:43.350 align:middle line:90%
is doing is--

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:45.540 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if I
completely agree with it--

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:48.820 align:middle line:84%
blending computer science and
computer literacy together.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:51.510 align:middle line:84%
I do think that teaching
computer literacy is

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.430 align:middle line:90%
of utmost importance.

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:59.430 align:middle line:84%
I credit a lot of my computer
literacy to my seventh grade--

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:03.060 align:middle line:84%
or seventh and eighth
grade technology teacher.

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:06.377 align:middle line:84%
The LinkedIn I use now was
created back in seventh grade,

00:27:06.377 --> 00:27:07.960 align:middle line:84%
and I still use the
very same account.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:09.990 align:middle line:90%
And he was the one who got me--

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:12.350 align:middle line:84%
I think I mentioned
the typing lessons

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:13.600 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of the class.

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:15.270 align:middle line:84%
That's where I
learned how to type.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:17.710 align:middle line:84%
That's where I learned
how to create a portfolio,

00:27:17.710 --> 00:27:22.210 align:middle line:84%
create an online website, do
individual kind of research,

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:23.960 align:middle line:84%
and create a project
on something that I'm

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:25.340 align:middle line:90%
interested about.

00:27:25.340 --> 00:27:28.220 align:middle line:84%
And I think creating
projects on something that

00:27:28.220 --> 00:27:33.668 align:middle line:84%
is personal to someone
within a K through 12 lesson

00:27:33.668 --> 00:27:35.210 align:middle line:84%
is really important
for teaching them

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:38.720 align:middle line:84%
how to conduct research,
take notes, and come up

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:42.390 align:middle line:84%
with conclusions on what they
got up and looked for online.

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:44.780 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And a constant
challenge educators have is,

00:27:44.780 --> 00:27:48.410 align:middle line:84%
what new things are
actually worth investing

00:27:48.410 --> 00:27:49.920 align:middle line:84%
additional
discretionary time in?

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:52.380 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there were plenty of
folks 10 years ago who said,

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:54.842 align:middle line:84%
man, it's incredibly
important-- every high schooler

00:27:54.842 --> 00:27:55.800 align:middle line:90%
should complete a MOOC.

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:58.190 align:middle line:84%
MOOCs are going to be absolutely
central to how people learn

00:27:58.190 --> 00:27:59.700 align:middle line:84%
and finish higher
education in the future.

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:02.158 align:middle line:84%
If you can't do a MOOC, you're
really going to be left out.

00:28:02.158 --> 00:28:03.950 align:middle line:84%
It's like, well,
maybe not so much.

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:05.920 align:middle line:84%
A LinkedIn profile,
however, many years later,

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:07.837 align:middle line:84%
still seems like a pretty
handy thing to have.

00:28:07.837 --> 00:28:10.760 align:middle line:84%
There was no reason, 10
years ago, that you--

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:12.260 align:middle line:84%
it's very easy to
imagine a world

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:14.838 align:middle line:84%
where LinkedIn got replaced
by something else entirely

00:28:14.838 --> 00:28:16.130 align:middle line:90%
and MOOCs were super important.

00:28:16.130 --> 00:28:18.400 align:middle line:84%
In 2013, the success
paths of those things

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:20.770 align:middle line:90%
were not at all predictable.

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:22.550 align:middle line:84%
Educators were super
anxious about--

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:23.270 align:middle line:90%
I just gave it--

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:24.350 align:middle line:84%
Erin came to this
conference with me.

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:26.860 align:middle line:84%
I gave a talk to a bunch of
educators about AI and education

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:27.940 align:middle line:90%
on Monday.

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:30.520 align:middle line:84%
And there's a ton
of anxiety about,

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:31.970 align:middle line:90%
how important are these things?

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:33.020 align:middle line:90%
What do we not need?

00:28:33.020 --> 00:28:34.895 align:middle line:84%
Does it make sense to
teach kids the same way

00:28:34.895 --> 00:28:36.700 align:middle line:84%
to search anymore if
AI agents are just

00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:37.970 align:middle line:84%
going to answer our
questions for us,

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:40.470 align:middle line:84%
or is it more important to be
able to do that because the AI

00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:42.550 align:middle line:84%
agents are going to
say nonsense to us?

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:43.820 align:middle line:90%
All these kinds of things.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:44.457 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Ty?

00:28:44.457 --> 00:28:45.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, sorry.

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:47.920 align:middle line:84%
In that regard,
do you consider AI

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:50.595 align:middle line:84%
a form of peer
learning because it's

00:28:50.595 --> 00:28:51.970 align:middle line:84%
been trained on
peer communities,

00:28:51.970 --> 00:28:53.530 align:middle line:84%
like Stack Overflow and
all these other things?

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:54.988 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I
love that question.

00:28:54.988 --> 00:28:57.520 align:middle line:90%
So my first thing about AI--

00:28:57.520 --> 00:29:00.492 align:middle line:84%
I was giving a talk
with Jim Waldo,

00:29:00.492 --> 00:29:02.450 align:middle line:84%
who was the chief technical
officer at Harvard.

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:05.800 align:middle line:84%
He's a neat guy because
he got a PhD in philosophy

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:08.480 align:middle line:84%
about 40 years ago, then
went and worked at Oracle,

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:09.500 align:middle line:90%
helped write Java.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:10.690 align:middle line:84%
If you have an Android
phone, you're probably

00:29:10.690 --> 00:29:12.020 align:middle line:90%
running his code on it.

00:29:12.020 --> 00:29:13.840 align:middle line:90%
And then came back.

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:16.490 align:middle line:84%
And now he does policy stuff,
and he's a philosopher again.

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:20.700 align:middle line:84%
But he's like, AI is what we
call computing technologies that

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:23.100 align:middle line:84%
are new and we don't
understand very well.

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:26.130 align:middle line:84%
And 10 years from now, they
won't be called AI anymore.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:30.970 align:middle line:84%
And so are generative
pre-trained transformers

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:32.200 align:middle line:90%
peer communication?

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:35.310 align:middle line:90%
Well, maybe.

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:39.090 align:middle line:84%
What do I think a
peer-guided network is?

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:42.360 align:middle line:84%
A learner enters a
network with a wide range

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:44.610 align:middle line:84%
of learning resources
that are generated

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:47.730 align:middle line:90%
by previously all human agents.

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:50.760 align:middle line:84%
Could a generative
pre-trained transformer

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:53.380 align:middle line:84%
generate a useful
learning sequence?

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:56.410 align:middle line:90%
Seems plausible.

00:29:56.410 --> 00:29:59.010 align:middle line:90%
I remain not entirely convinced.

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:03.280 align:middle line:84%
Actually, somebody came
up to me and was like,

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:05.550 align:middle line:84%
we have a startup where
we're creating online classes

00:30:05.550 --> 00:30:07.600 align:middle line:90%
by having AI create the classes.

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:10.620 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, that just sounds
like a terrible idea, in part

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:13.030 align:middle line:84%
because there's just such
a huge surfeit of classes.

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:14.070 align:middle line:84%
There's so many
things out there,

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:15.653 align:middle line:84%
and they're really
not, unfortunately,

00:30:15.653 --> 00:30:17.180 align:middle line:90%
that expensive to develop.

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:21.140 align:middle line:84%
Why do you need
machines to do them?

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:26.400 align:middle line:84%
I have a colleague,
Carolyn Rosé,

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:30.650 align:middle line:84%
who worked with
chatbots for decades.

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:34.370 align:middle line:84%
And she actually
has convinced me

00:30:34.370 --> 00:30:37.910 align:middle line:84%
to be very skeptical of
interactions of individuals

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:39.180 align:middle line:90%
with chatbots.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:40.070 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:42.403 align:middle line:84%
How many of you in the last
week spent more than an hour

00:30:42.403 --> 00:30:43.940 align:middle line:90%
talking to a chatbot?

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:44.720 align:middle line:90%
Zero.

00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:46.820 align:middle line:90%
Maybe one.

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:48.770 align:middle line:84%
I just don't think
people like it that much.

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:51.680 align:middle line:84%
She really thought that
where they could be powerful

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:54.230 align:middle line:84%
is jumping in in the
conversations between two

00:30:54.230 --> 00:30:57.270 align:middle line:84%
people, that you just have
peers talking to each other.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:30:58.700 align:middle line:84%
So you're on this
discussion forum

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:01.117 align:middle line:84%
and you're going back and forth
or other things like that,

00:31:01.117 --> 00:31:02.840 align:middle line:84%
and then a bot comes
in and helps you

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:04.245 align:middle line:90%
ask an interesting question.

00:31:04.245 --> 00:31:06.620 align:middle line:84%
Hey, look, somebody over here
has a different perspective

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:07.020 align:middle line:90%
on that.

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:07.890 align:middle line:90%
Let me connect you to that.

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:09.830 align:middle line:84%
And so you can talk
and things like that.

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:13.600 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, I think it
totally seems plausible

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:17.535 align:middle line:84%
that a peer-guided learning
environment could include

00:31:17.535 --> 00:31:19.660 align:middle line:84%
artificial agents that are
doing things that humans

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:22.060 align:middle line:90%
had previously been doing.

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:25.070 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I guess, to both
your points of AI interaction,

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:30.770 align:middle line:84%
I would say an interesting
aspect of that,

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:33.040 align:middle line:84%
especially in terms
of what can be

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:35.300 align:middle line:84%
a credible source for
learning and stuff like that.

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:39.980 align:middle line:84%
So I think, in California, or
at least the part of California

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:41.980 align:middle line:84%
I was in for a
while, we did have,

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:45.400 align:middle line:84%
in middle school,
a week-long program

00:31:45.400 --> 00:31:49.480 align:middle line:84%
that every kid had to do
that was like, oh, you're 13.

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:50.900 align:middle line:90%
Here, make an email.

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:54.040 align:middle line:90%
And here's how you Google stuff.

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:58.180 align:middle line:84%
And recently, with
the new Google

00:31:58.180 --> 00:32:00.250 align:middle line:84%
AI feature that
kind of summarizes

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:03.880 align:middle line:84%
answers for you now,
I was thinking about,

00:32:03.880 --> 00:32:08.510 align:middle line:84%
how does that change those
levels of instructions?

00:32:08.510 --> 00:32:11.200 align:middle line:84%
Because sometimes I look up
things, and I see the summary,

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.380 align:middle line:84%
and I'm like, I don't
know what the answer is,

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:14.720 align:middle line:90%
but I know it's not that.

00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:16.340 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:18.450 align:middle line:90%
And I go deeper.

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:21.810 align:middle line:84%
But let's say, if I'm a
middle schooler and it's like,

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:24.170 align:middle line:84%
well, I don't learn this,
or I'm a high schooler

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:25.770 align:middle line:84%
that took that class
in middle school.

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:28.080 align:middle line:84%
And it's like, well,
now I'm not sure.

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:30.818 align:middle line:90%
Or I take Bard's word for good.

00:32:30.818 --> 00:32:31.610 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:32.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:35.030 align:middle line:84%
No, a huge problem
with GPT-generated text

00:32:35.030 --> 00:32:37.400 align:middle line:84%
is that it's probably much
more useful for experts

00:32:37.400 --> 00:32:38.780 align:middle line:90%
than for novices.

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:43.248 align:middle line:84%
So if you ask a GPT to make
eight things and two of them

00:32:43.248 --> 00:32:45.540 align:middle line:84%
are cool and six of them are
nonsense, or the opposite,

00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:46.970 align:middle line:84%
an expert can tell
the difference

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:49.050 align:middle line:84%
and be like, oh, that was an
idea I didn't have before.

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:49.980 align:middle line:90%
But a novice can't.

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:52.050 align:middle line:84%
A novice just looks and be
like, OK, these are eight ideas.

00:32:52.050 --> 00:32:53.690 align:middle line:84%
They all seem plausible
and they're confidently

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:54.780 align:middle line:90%
stated and things like that.

00:32:54.780 --> 00:32:56.210 align:middle line:84%
It's a huge problem
that educators

00:32:56.210 --> 00:32:59.770 align:middle line:84%
are going to have to wrestle
with in the years ahead.

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:02.620 align:middle line:90%


00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:06.740 align:middle line:84%
The kind of class that
you're describing,

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:09.960 align:middle line:84%
if it were to be
taught 10 years ago,

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:13.020 align:middle line:84%
a big thing that educators
were thinking about at the time

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:17.190 align:middle line:84%
was, how are people going
to navigate a social web?

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:19.865 align:middle line:84%
So when the web was
first developed--

00:33:19.865 --> 00:33:21.490 align:middle line:84%
we'll get into this
a little bit more--

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:24.000 align:middle line:84%
it was pretty hard for
people to contribute to it.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:25.720 align:middle line:84%
You had to know a
little bit about FTP,

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:27.527 align:middle line:84%
you had to know a
little bit about HTML,

00:33:27.527 --> 00:33:28.860 align:middle line:90%
a couple other things like that.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:30.540 align:middle line:84%
They weren't super
hard, but there

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:32.790 align:middle line:84%
was a relatively small
portion of the population that

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:35.070 align:middle line:90%
added content to the internet.

00:33:35.070 --> 00:33:37.170 align:middle line:84%
And there was a
suite of technologies

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:40.830 align:middle line:84%
that got bundled and called
Web 2.0, which said, OK, we're

00:33:40.830 --> 00:33:44.110 align:middle line:84%
going to make it much easier for
anybody to type things online.

00:33:44.110 --> 00:33:45.400 align:middle line:90%
You can all start a blog.

00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:46.810 align:middle line:90%
You can all start a microblog.

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:48.730 align:middle line:84%
You can all make
a simple website.

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:51.420 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to know how to
code to do any of these things.

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:53.880 align:middle line:84%
We're going to use
user interfaces

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:56.760 align:middle line:84%
to take away all that
programming expertise that's

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:57.520 align:middle line:90%
necessary.

00:33:57.520 --> 00:34:02.100 align:middle line:84%
And so people thought,
wow, I bet our lives

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:03.780 align:middle line:90%
and young people's lives--

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:06.707 align:middle line:84%
if you're teaching 11-year-olds,
12-year-olds in 2013,

00:34:06.707 --> 00:34:09.290 align:middle line:84%
you're like, man, a major thing
they're going to have to do is

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:10.650 align:middle line:84%
how to connect and
network with each other.

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:12.400 align:middle line:84%
We have to have them
create their LinkedIn

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:15.090 align:middle line:84%
page so that they know how to
social network with each other.

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:18.025 align:middle line:84%
We have to have them
participate in online dialogues.

00:34:18.025 --> 00:34:19.400 align:middle line:84%
We have to have
them think about,

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:22.350 align:middle line:84%
what are responsible ways
of representing themselves?

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:25.370 align:middle line:84%
People talk a lot about
their digital footprint

00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:28.429 align:middle line:84%
and other kinds of
things like that.

00:34:28.429 --> 00:34:34.130 align:middle line:84%
Today, a 13-year-old is entering
a web environment in which there

00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:37.880 align:middle line:84%
are lots of parts of social
media which are collapsing.

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:40.676 align:middle line:90%


00:34:40.676 --> 00:34:42.710 align:middle line:84%
Just as an example,
actually, I went

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:45.480 align:middle line:84%
to the guy who ran this
conference on Monday,

00:34:45.480 --> 00:34:47.639 align:middle line:90%
and it was kind of interesting.

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:50.760 align:middle line:84%
So usually this conference
get 100 or 150 people show up.

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:51.590 align:middle line:90%
It was about AI.

00:34:51.590 --> 00:34:54.980 align:middle line:84%
They had 240 people show up, and
70 folks were on the waitlist.

00:34:54.980 --> 00:34:56.833 align:middle line:84%
Tons and tons of
interest from educators.

00:34:56.833 --> 00:34:59.250 align:middle line:84%
And I went to the guy who runs
it, Tom Driscoll, and says,

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:00.780 align:middle line:84%
what do you think these
teachers are interested in?

00:35:00.780 --> 00:35:02.550 align:middle line:84%
What are you-- what
do they want to know?

00:35:02.550 --> 00:35:03.590 align:middle line:90%
Why are they here?

00:35:03.590 --> 00:35:05.100 align:middle line:90%
And he's like, you know what?

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:06.140 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:35:06.140 --> 00:35:09.620 align:middle line:84%
10 years ago, they
would have had blogs.

00:35:09.620 --> 00:35:11.595 align:middle line:84%
They would have
participated in forums.

00:35:11.595 --> 00:35:13.220 align:middle line:84%
They would have joined
Facebook groups.

00:35:13.220 --> 00:35:17.620 align:middle line:84%
They would have-- there
were many, many more ways

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:20.740 align:middle line:84%
that educators were
proactively contributing

00:35:20.740 --> 00:35:22.010 align:middle line:90%
their ideas to the web.

00:35:22.010 --> 00:35:23.060 align:middle line:90%
Maybe not all of them.

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:24.898 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it was 10% of
them or 20% of them,

00:35:24.898 --> 00:35:26.440 align:middle line:84%
but there was enough
of them that you

00:35:26.440 --> 00:35:30.250 align:middle line:84%
could get the pulse of
a whole community, which

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:33.670 align:middle line:84%
he felt like, and I agree,
you really can't do anymore.

00:35:33.670 --> 00:35:37.570 align:middle line:84%
Some of that is that
there was a moment

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:40.660 align:middle line:84%
in Web 2.0 in which
people believed

00:35:40.660 --> 00:35:42.790 align:middle line:84%
that the infrastructure
of the web

00:35:42.790 --> 00:35:46.930 align:middle line:84%
might be really quite
open and quite owned

00:35:46.930 --> 00:35:49.300 align:middle line:84%
or controlled by
individual people,

00:35:49.300 --> 00:35:51.820 align:middle line:84%
that there could be a
world where people create

00:35:51.820 --> 00:35:54.140 align:middle line:84%
their own websites, they
create their own blogs,

00:35:54.140 --> 00:35:56.830 align:middle line:84%
they host their own accounts,
and things like that.

00:35:56.830 --> 00:36:00.100 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know when some
of these things-- starting

00:36:00.100 --> 00:36:03.460 align:middle line:84%
around 2015, 2016,
people started

00:36:03.460 --> 00:36:08.700 align:middle line:84%
describing The Web We Lost,
a kind of moment in which

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:12.030 align:middle line:90%
social media took a turn.

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:14.400 align:middle line:84%
So these are the kinds
of things that, I

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:17.230 align:middle line:84%
don't know, I was playing
around with in 2004, 2007.

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:19.347 align:middle line:84%
Any of you have a
LiveJournal blog?

00:36:19.347 --> 00:36:21.180 align:middle line:84%
That was one of the
earliest blogging sites,

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:23.263 align:middle line:84%
that people create their
own space and hosting it.

00:36:23.263 --> 00:36:26.427 align:middle line:84%
This was a wordpress.com
blog of my wife and I,

00:36:26.427 --> 00:36:29.010 align:middle line:84%
writing little stories about us
going on adventures and things

00:36:29.010 --> 00:36:32.400 align:middle line:84%
like that, posting pictures,
hosting it ourselves.

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:36.540 align:middle line:84%
And essentially, Facebook
came and demolished all this

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:38.700 align:middle line:90%
and said, you know what?

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:40.870 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to
host your own blog.

00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:45.630 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to
figure out an FTP server

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:48.270 align:middle line:84%
to upload your own pictures
or use a picture interface.

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:50.250 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
create an interface.

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:52.740 align:middle line:84%
People participate in a lot
of the same kind of practices

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:55.200 align:middle line:84%
on Facebook, of writing little
stories on the trips they do

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:56.908 align:middle line:84%
and posting pictures
and stuff like that.

00:36:56.908 --> 00:37:01.560 align:middle line:84%
But all of a sudden, it was all
controlled by Mark Zuckerberg.

00:37:01.560 --> 00:37:03.648 align:middle line:84%
This was a quote from
David Weinberger.

00:37:03.648 --> 00:37:05.440 align:middle line:84%
"In many ways, Facebook
fulfilled the dream

00:37:05.440 --> 00:37:06.140 align:middle line:90%
of blogging."

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:07.940 align:middle line:90%
But not exactly.

00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:10.930 align:middle line:84%
Everybody was online, but
they weren't in spaces

00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:15.700 align:middle line:84%
that they owned, had control
over, had some familiarity with.

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:19.330 align:middle line:84%
And then-- we'll talk about
some of this as it goes along--

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:23.740 align:middle line:84%
I think there was a moment
in which participation

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:29.500 align:middle line:84%
in a broad online life felt
much less toxic and draining

00:37:29.500 --> 00:37:34.210 align:middle line:84%
and vulnerability-inspiring
as it does right now.

00:37:34.210 --> 00:37:38.680 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you're
a teacher who's

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:40.360 align:middle line:84%
working in a state
which has recently

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:43.718 align:middle line:84%
passed a divisive concepts
law or other things like that,

00:37:43.718 --> 00:37:45.760 align:middle line:84%
regulating the kinds of
topics you can talk about

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:50.080 align:middle line:84%
and things like that, all of
that has a chilling effect.

00:37:50.080 --> 00:37:54.683 align:middle line:84%
I mean, not to mention just the
ways in which the social media

00:37:54.683 --> 00:37:57.100 align:middle line:84%
services, Facebook, Twitter,
other kinds of things we use,

00:37:57.100 --> 00:38:01.990 align:middle line:84%
have degraded in their
conversations over time.

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:04.050 align:middle line:84%
This is what David
Weinberger said.

00:38:04.050 --> 00:38:08.120 align:middle line:90%
"The blogosphere did not scale."

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:11.140 align:middle line:84%
Some people created their
own blogs, but not everyone.

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:14.117 align:middle line:84%
And then Facebook came in, and
many people who created blogs

00:38:14.117 --> 00:38:15.700 align:middle line:84%
were like, I'll just
post to Facebook.

00:38:15.700 --> 00:38:17.242 align:middle line:84%
A lot of those people
have moved over

00:38:17.242 --> 00:38:20.040 align:middle line:84%
to Substack now or other kinds
of things like that, but systems

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:22.440 align:middle line:90%
that they don't control.

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:26.280 align:middle line:84%
There is, I think, a
parallel to that story that

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:29.010 align:middle line:90%
happened in higher education.

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:34.200 align:middle line:84%
So in the early days, when the
web was first made available

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:38.050 align:middle line:84%
broadly to schools, which also
happens to overlap with my life.

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:42.030 align:middle line:84%
So I think, in 1993, Dartmouth
College is the first college

00:38:42.030 --> 00:38:46.060 align:middle line:84%
to give every incoming
student an email address.

00:38:46.060 --> 00:38:50.880 align:middle line:84%
I think, in 1994, very quickly,
many, many other colleges

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:51.540 align:middle line:90%
had done that.

00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:53.740 align:middle line:84%
By the time I
graduated high school,

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:56.760 align:middle line:84%
but certainly by the time
I was into college in 1996,

00:38:56.760 --> 00:38:57.970 align:middle line:90%
I had an email address.

00:38:57.970 --> 00:38:59.928 align:middle line:84%
Everybody else would have
had an email address.

00:38:59.928 --> 00:39:02.630 align:middle line:84%
That would have been pretty
universal and standard.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:07.040 align:middle line:84%
But this is what university web
pages looked like at the time.

00:39:07.040 --> 00:39:09.330 align:middle line:84%
MIT still has some of
these kinds of things.

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:11.370 align:middle line:84%
Has anybody ever been to
somebody's Tilde Space?

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:16.530 align:middle line:84%
www.mit.edu/ tildejreich
or something like that.

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:18.240 align:middle line:90%
Who do you--

00:39:18.240 --> 00:39:18.990 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, man.

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:20.280 align:middle line:90%
The 18.06 professor.

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:22.314 align:middle line:90%
What's his name?

00:39:22.314 --> 00:39:24.380 align:middle line:90%
I keep forgetting.

00:39:24.380 --> 00:39:25.438 align:middle line:90%
The one who just retired.

00:39:25.438 --> 00:39:26.480 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Gilbert Strang?

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:27.060 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Gil Strang?

00:39:27.060 --> 00:39:27.320 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:27.780 align:middle line:90%
Perfect.

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:28.280 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:39:28.280 --> 00:39:31.910 align:middle line:84%
Others that people can think
of who have these things?

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:34.178 align:middle line:84%
So some things that you'll
notice about the-- well,

00:39:34.178 --> 00:39:34.720 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:35.990 align:middle line:84%
What are you-- just looking
at these pictures, what do

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:37.020 align:middle line:90%
you notice about these things?

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:38.190 align:middle line:90%
What strikes you about them?

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:40.732 align:middle line:84%
There were some giggles before,
which I think is appropriate.

00:39:40.732 --> 00:39:44.330 align:middle line:90%


00:39:44.330 --> 00:39:47.000 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Everything
is Times New Roman.

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:47.920 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK.

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:50.030 align:middle line:90%
So not a lot of font diversity.

00:39:50.030 --> 00:39:50.845 align:middle line:90%
What else?

00:39:50.845 --> 00:39:54.500 align:middle line:90%


00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:56.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's
no advertisements.

00:39:56.180 --> 00:39:57.222 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, good.

00:39:57.222 --> 00:40:00.420 align:middle line:84%
No, so these were not
created with Wix or something

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:03.895 align:middle line:84%
like that, where, in exchange
for making a website,

00:40:03.895 --> 00:40:05.520 align:middle line:84%
you have to put a
bar of advertisements

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:06.700 align:middle line:84%
at the bottom or
something like that.

00:40:06.700 --> 00:40:08.500 align:middle line:84%
So somebody actually
made these pages.

00:40:08.500 --> 00:40:10.140 align:middle line:90%
They're all personal home pages.

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.750 align:middle line:90%
Other things that strike you?

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:14.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The backgrounds.

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:17.550 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: What
about the backgrounds?

00:40:17.550 --> 00:40:18.780 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's certainly of--

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:19.570 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:40:19.570 --> 00:40:21.810 align:middle line:84%
I feel like I personally
associate them

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:24.252 align:middle line:84%
with a very specific
time period.

00:40:24.252 --> 00:40:25.210 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK, good.

00:40:25.210 --> 00:40:26.627 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like
this is naturally--

00:40:26.627 --> 00:40:28.870 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: They have
a '90s aesthetic to them?

00:40:28.870 --> 00:40:29.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:40:29.530 --> 00:40:32.970 align:middle line:84%
I would say maybe
it is partially

00:40:32.970 --> 00:40:40.110 align:middle line:84%
just the quality of, let's say,
a pattern or something you can

00:40:40.110 --> 00:40:42.750 align:middle line:84%
put on, or now we have
a lot of templates

00:40:42.750 --> 00:40:45.310 align:middle line:84%
and make sure it's
aesthetically consistent.

00:40:45.310 --> 00:40:47.518 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: This is pre-Canva.

00:40:47.518 --> 00:40:49.060 align:middle line:84%
Kent, what were you
going to observe?

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:50.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was about to
say about the templates.

00:40:50.430 --> 00:40:52.798 align:middle line:84%
They're not all using
the exact same template

00:40:52.798 --> 00:40:53.590 align:middle line:90%
and similar format.

00:40:53.590 --> 00:40:56.190 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: They're not all
using the exact same template

00:40:56.190 --> 00:40:57.400 align:middle line:90%
and similar formatting.

00:40:57.400 --> 00:41:00.590 align:middle line:84%
I think that that is
actually quite important.

00:41:00.590 --> 00:41:03.320 align:middle line:84%
There are some good
things that templates do,

00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:07.520 align:middle line:84%
of make things
looking aesthetic,

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:09.660 align:middle line:90%
but they standardize in a way.

00:41:09.660 --> 00:41:11.370 align:middle line:90%
They put things in the same way.

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:13.760 align:middle line:84%
People at universities do
really, really different kinds

00:41:13.760 --> 00:41:14.180 align:middle line:90%
of things.

00:41:14.180 --> 00:41:15.180 align:middle line:90%
That might be important.

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:17.240 align:middle line:90%
Other observations about these?

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:19.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's
very skeuomorphic.

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:22.190 align:middle line:84%
There's buttons on the bottom
left, along those icons,

00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:24.647 align:middle line:90%
that you don't see as often.

00:41:24.647 --> 00:41:26.480 align:middle line:84%
And I want to say the
background, especially

00:41:26.480 --> 00:41:31.310 align:middle line:84%
the bottom left, where I think
of a physical wallpaper, which

00:41:31.310 --> 00:41:32.605 align:middle line:90%
is like the principal--

00:41:32.605 --> 00:41:34.980 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Do you want to
define skeuomorphism for us?

00:41:34.980 --> 00:41:37.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE:
Skeuomorphism is like--

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:40.290 align:middle line:84%
the most famous example
is probably iOS 6,

00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:43.940 align:middle line:84%
where you have all these
buttons and reflections that

00:41:43.940 --> 00:41:45.420 align:middle line:90%
try to mimic real life.

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:50.070 align:middle line:84%
And that's what skeuomorphism
is, trying to mimic real life.

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:52.380 align:middle line:84%
And then, when we
transitioned to iOS 7,

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:54.470 align:middle line:84%
you removed a lot
of that dimension,

00:41:54.470 --> 00:41:57.007 align:middle line:84%
and it became a lot more
simplistic and symbolic.

00:41:57.007 --> 00:41:58.840 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: A lot of
the early note taking

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:02.450 align:middle line:84%
apps would have a background
like a yellow legal pad,

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:05.120 align:middle line:84%
or a spiral binding on the
side or something like that.

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:06.790 align:middle line:84%
And you're like,
brother, we could put

00:42:06.790 --> 00:42:08.380 align:middle line:90%
the-- we don't need the lines.

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:10.330 align:middle line:90%
The cursor will do that for you.

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:13.840 align:middle line:84%
But people wanted to make
some kind of connection

00:42:13.840 --> 00:42:15.140 align:middle line:90%
between what they saw--

00:42:15.140 --> 00:42:16.640 align:middle line:84%
what they saw in
the physical world,

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:18.230 align:middle line:84%
and what this new digital
thing was trying to do.

00:42:18.230 --> 00:42:20.210 align:middle line:84%
Skeuomorphism shows up
in that book somewhere.

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:22.043 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember now
where I wrote about it.

00:42:22.043 --> 00:42:23.990 align:middle line:90%
But yeah.

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:28.990 align:middle line:84%
I mean, obviously, these
pictures are just outstanding.

00:42:28.990 --> 00:42:36.640 align:middle line:84%
Just like people had kind
of a cool, idiosyncratic,

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:39.310 align:middle line:84%
personalized, kind
of a pain in the butt

00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:43.210 align:middle line:84%
to organize blogosphere that
existed, that Facebook just

00:42:43.210 --> 00:42:46.750 align:middle line:84%
came and demolished and
homogenized and standardized

00:42:46.750 --> 00:42:49.960 align:middle line:84%
and pulled out of the control
of individual people's spaces

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:54.310 align:middle line:84%
and into this corporate space,
you could say that Blackboard

00:42:54.310 --> 00:42:57.690 align:middle line:84%
did something very, very
similar to campus web pages,

00:42:57.690 --> 00:43:01.450 align:middle line:84%
that learning management systems
came along and they said,

00:43:01.450 --> 00:43:03.420 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
standardize it all.

00:43:03.420 --> 00:43:06.690 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to create
your own page anymore.

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:08.770 align:middle line:84%
Blackboard will create
your own faculty page,

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:10.890 align:middle line:90%
create your own course page.

00:43:10.890 --> 00:43:13.350 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to come
with some kind of benefits

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:14.260 align:middle line:90%
of standardization.

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:16.177 align:middle line:84%
It's going to make it a
lot easier for people.

00:43:16.177 --> 00:43:19.980 align:middle line:84%
But it's also going
to get rid of some

00:43:19.980 --> 00:43:23.950 align:middle line:84%
of the wonderful
idiosyncrasies of these things.

00:43:23.950 --> 00:43:27.990 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to mean that
there is a corporate entity who

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:30.120 align:middle line:84%
is not accountable--
who's accountable to their

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:32.460 align:middle line:84%
shareholders and not
accountable to the same values

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:35.460 align:middle line:84%
that we might have as a
university, which is deciding

00:43:35.460 --> 00:43:37.800 align:middle line:84%
what those things look like,
much like when we move all

00:43:37.800 --> 00:43:40.993 align:middle line:84%
of our blogs onto
Facebook, you're not even

00:43:40.993 --> 00:43:42.160 align:middle line:90%
accountable to shareholders.

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:45.180 align:middle line:84%
You're just accountable to
whatever Mark thinks people

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:47.640 align:middle line:90%
should be doing in Facebook.

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:48.160 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:48.160 --> 00:43:49.590 align:middle line:84%
In many ways,
Blackboard fulfilled

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:50.757 align:middle line:90%
the dream of the university.

00:43:50.757 --> 00:43:51.895 align:middle line:90%
Tilde Spaces did not scale.

00:43:51.895 --> 00:43:53.770 align:middle line:84%
I believe the problem
could have been solved.

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:55.300 align:middle line:90%
But then Blackboard happened.

00:43:55.300 --> 00:43:57.490 align:middle line:90%
Why would Blackboard happen?

00:43:57.490 --> 00:44:01.750 align:middle line:84%
These are the instructions
for SUNY Oneonta

00:44:01.750 --> 00:44:04.830 align:middle line:84%
about how you would go about
creating one of your Tilde Space

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:05.330 align:middle line:90%
pages.

00:44:05.330 --> 00:44:06.950 align:middle line:90%
Log in to ACAD.

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:07.970 align:middle line:90%
Create a directory.

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:09.140 align:middle line:90%
Change to this directory.

00:44:09.140 --> 00:44:11.350 align:middle line:90%
Create HTML documents.

00:44:11.350 --> 00:44:12.950 align:middle line:90%
HTML Developers Jump Station.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:16.370 align:middle line:84%
Set prot equals w
colon r filename x.

00:44:16.370 --> 00:44:19.460 align:middle line:84%
You read about a page of this
stuff and you're like, yeah,

00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:21.250 align:middle line:90%
I can see why a lot of people--

00:44:21.250 --> 00:44:24.390 align:middle line:84%
why launching a Blackboard
page might be fine.

00:44:24.390 --> 00:44:27.760 align:middle line:90%


00:44:27.760 --> 00:44:30.565 align:middle line:90%
This is what the--

00:44:30.565 --> 00:44:33.280 align:middle line:90%
was this Blackboard?

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:37.580 align:middle line:84%
I don't remember what learning
management system this was.

00:44:37.580 --> 00:44:38.990 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's Canvas.

00:44:38.990 --> 00:44:39.670 align:middle line:90%
It's not Canvas.

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:41.545 align:middle line:84%
I don't know I can't
remember what it is now.

00:44:41.545 --> 00:44:46.510 align:middle line:84%
This is the very first version
of a version of this course

00:44:46.510 --> 00:44:48.370 align:middle line:90%
that I taught 10 years ago.

00:44:48.370 --> 00:44:53.490 align:middle line:84%
And this is the page that
the system generated for me

00:44:53.490 --> 00:44:55.240 align:middle line:90%
when I first created the class.

00:44:55.240 --> 00:44:58.020 align:middle line:84%
So it comes with a bunch of
these sort of-- not this stuff.

00:44:58.020 --> 00:44:58.720 align:middle line:90%
That I added.

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:01.120 align:middle line:84%
But Syllabus, Class
Sessions, Other Resources,

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:03.430 align:middle line:84%
Assignments, Collaborate,
Information, Reserves Lists,

00:45:03.430 --> 00:45:05.280 align:middle line:90%
Search this Site, Lecture Video.

00:45:05.280 --> 00:45:08.580 align:middle line:84%
It makes a set of assumptions
about what my course is.

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:11.910 align:middle line:84%
It says, this is what the
standard format of this learning

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:13.860 align:middle line:90%
experience should be.

00:45:13.860 --> 00:45:16.955 align:middle line:84%
But maybe that's not
at all what I want.

00:45:16.955 --> 00:45:18.330 align:middle line:84%
It certainly
doesn't make a lot--

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:21.180 align:middle line:84%
if I had taught
this class about--

00:45:21.180 --> 00:45:22.750 align:middle line:90%
this was in an education school.

00:45:22.750 --> 00:45:25.570 align:middle line:84%
If I had made this class
about education technology,

00:45:25.570 --> 00:45:28.170 align:middle line:84%
about the experience
of Indigenous kids

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:31.600 align:middle line:84%
learning on reservations,
about, I don't know,

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:36.760 align:middle line:84%
the global education policy,
intermediate statistics,

00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:38.970 align:middle line:84%
it all would have been
the exact same format.

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:41.940 align:middle line:84%
They would have given the
same standardized creation

00:45:41.940 --> 00:45:44.170 align:middle line:90%
for this, which in lots of--

00:45:44.170 --> 00:45:45.670 align:middle line:84%
it makes sense in
some kind of ways,

00:45:45.670 --> 00:45:47.080 align:middle line:84%
and it's bonkers and
other kinds of ways.

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:48.532 align:middle line:90%
Our courses are super different.

00:45:48.532 --> 00:45:50.740 align:middle line:84%
Why should all of our courses
have the same template?

00:45:50.740 --> 00:45:53.900 align:middle line:84%
Why should we all be
subtly, invisibly squished

00:45:53.900 --> 00:45:56.600 align:middle line:84%
into teaching our
courses the same way

00:45:56.600 --> 00:45:59.540 align:middle line:90%
by these kinds of structures?

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:04.220 align:middle line:84%
And of course, there's all kinds
of physical analogs to this too.

00:46:04.220 --> 00:46:10.008 align:middle line:84%
The way we design environments
shapes the learning experiences

00:46:10.008 --> 00:46:11.300 align:middle line:90%
that happen in the environment.

00:46:11.300 --> 00:46:13.140 align:middle line:84%
I just read a beautiful
sentence of this,

00:46:13.140 --> 00:46:15.020 align:middle line:90%
which is something like--

00:46:15.020 --> 00:46:18.950 align:middle line:84%
it was something like, buildings
have agenda for their occupants.

00:46:18.950 --> 00:46:22.350 align:middle line:84%
The way that spaces are created,
it doesn't tell you what to do,

00:46:22.350 --> 00:46:24.030 align:middle line:90%
but it certainly guides you.

00:46:24.030 --> 00:46:26.810 align:middle line:84%
You walk into a room
like this and you

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:28.460 align:middle line:84%
have some thoughts
about the kind

00:46:28.460 --> 00:46:30.502 align:middle line:84%
of learning that's going
to happen in that space.

00:46:30.502 --> 00:46:32.918 align:middle line:84%
Well, you're all going to be
facing in the same direction,

00:46:32.918 --> 00:46:35.540 align:middle line:84%
probably not going to be talking
to each other a whole lot

00:46:35.540 --> 00:46:37.205 align:middle line:90%
in that space.

00:46:37.205 --> 00:46:39.080 align:middle line:84%
You're going to be
writing with certain kinds

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:40.380 align:middle line:90%
of physical materials.

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:44.270 align:middle line:84%
If you try to use a laptop
on those square tables,

00:46:44.270 --> 00:46:45.270 align:middle line:90%
you'll have a hard time.

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:47.210 align:middle line:84%
There's no room for power
cords or other kinds

00:46:47.210 --> 00:46:48.260 align:middle line:90%
of things like this.

00:46:48.260 --> 00:46:50.310 align:middle line:84%
This is actually one of
the most famous examples

00:46:50.310 --> 00:46:53.460 align:middle line:84%
in elite private education
in the United States,

00:46:53.460 --> 00:47:01.170 align:middle line:84%
which is, there were donors
to the Phillips Exeter School

00:47:01.170 --> 00:47:05.668 align:middle line:84%
who were really concerned
that, essentially, there's

00:47:05.668 --> 00:47:07.960 align:middle line:84%
too much instructionism and
not enough constructionism.

00:47:07.960 --> 00:47:09.460 align:middle line:84%
They wouldn't have said
it the exact same way,

00:47:09.460 --> 00:47:10.380 align:middle line:84%
but they would
have said something

00:47:10.380 --> 00:47:11.922 align:middle line:84%
along those lines--
too much sitting,

00:47:11.922 --> 00:47:15.570 align:middle line:84%
passive listening, not
enough engaged conversation.

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:20.140 align:middle line:84%
And so a bunch of donors
got together and they said,

00:47:20.140 --> 00:47:22.290 align:middle line:84%
in every classroom,
we're going to pull out

00:47:22.290 --> 00:47:24.190 align:middle line:84%
all of the chairs
that look like this,

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:27.090 align:middle line:84%
and we're going to put in these
tables, called Harkness tables.

00:47:27.090 --> 00:47:29.070 align:middle line:84%
Some of the features
of Harkness tables

00:47:29.070 --> 00:47:31.020 align:middle line:84%
are that you need to be
able to fit everybody

00:47:31.020 --> 00:47:34.140 align:middle line:84%
in the class in an
oval around the table.

00:47:34.140 --> 00:47:36.360 align:middle line:84%
The tables are so
large that they usually

00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:39.317 align:middle line:84%
have to be built
in different pieces

00:47:39.317 --> 00:47:41.400 align:middle line:84%
and then shipped into the
room, and then assembled

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:42.580 align:middle line:90%
inside the room.

00:47:42.580 --> 00:47:46.120 align:middle line:84%
They're incredibly
heavy, but they make it

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:49.250 align:middle line:84%
so there is no
front of the room.

00:47:49.250 --> 00:47:53.790 align:middle line:84%
And at Phillips Exeter and other
kinds of independent schools,

00:47:53.790 --> 00:47:56.503 align:middle line:84%
they put these tables
in every classroom.

00:47:56.503 --> 00:47:58.170 align:middle line:84%
They put them in the
English classrooms,

00:47:58.170 --> 00:47:59.190 align:middle line:84%
they put them in the
history classrooms,

00:47:59.190 --> 00:48:00.450 align:middle line:84%
but they put them in
the math classrooms,

00:48:00.450 --> 00:48:02.150 align:middle line:84%
they put them in the
science classrooms.

00:48:02.150 --> 00:48:08.060 align:middle line:84%
And the intention was to change
the physical space in order

00:48:08.060 --> 00:48:10.340 align:middle line:84%
to create certain kinds
of learning environments

00:48:10.340 --> 00:48:12.210 align:middle line:84%
and certain kinds of
interactions in there.

00:48:12.210 --> 00:48:15.830 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, you
can give a math lecture

00:48:15.830 --> 00:48:20.160 align:middle line:84%
in a room like this, but it's
hard to lecture for 52 minutes

00:48:20.160 --> 00:48:22.820 align:middle line:84%
if you're standing here
and half the kids either

00:48:22.820 --> 00:48:25.910 align:middle line:84%
have their backs to you or have
to rotate around or other kinds

00:48:25.910 --> 00:48:27.140 align:middle line:90%
of things like that.

00:48:27.140 --> 00:48:29.460 align:middle line:84%
It's designed to
make people do that.

00:48:29.460 --> 00:48:36.860 align:middle line:84%
So you can think
about, what agenda

00:48:36.860 --> 00:48:40.070 align:middle line:84%
does this online space
have for its occupants?

00:48:40.070 --> 00:48:44.090 align:middle line:84%
What is it that a
generic Canvas classroom

00:48:44.090 --> 00:48:49.570 align:middle line:84%
tells instructors and students
that they're supposed to do?

00:48:49.570 --> 00:48:50.990 align:middle line:84%
You can look at
these links later,

00:48:50.990 --> 00:48:53.410 align:middle line:84%
but these are two
of the folks who,

00:48:53.410 --> 00:48:57.890 align:middle line:84%
as Blackboard was
sweeping higher education,

00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:03.280 align:middle line:84%
were amongst the
most active in trying

00:49:03.280 --> 00:49:04.700 align:middle line:90%
to have these conversations.

00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:06.640 align:middle line:84%
There was a book
that was written

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:10.540 align:middle line:84%
about them and some other
people called Edupunks

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:13.900 align:middle line:84%
and trying to make connection
between the punk movement

00:49:13.900 --> 00:49:20.380 align:middle line:84%
and music, very
countercultural, do-it-yourself,

00:49:20.380 --> 00:49:23.230 align:middle line:84%
kind of stand up to
the man sort of thing.

00:49:23.230 --> 00:49:26.330 align:middle line:84%
And folks like the "Reverend"
Jim Groom and Alan "Cogdog"

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:26.830 align:middle line:90%
Levine.

00:49:26.830 --> 00:49:29.620 align:middle line:84%
They have these monikers because
if you were participating

00:49:29.620 --> 00:49:31.330 align:middle line:84%
in social networks,
you create handles

00:49:31.330 --> 00:49:32.450 align:middle line:90%
for those kinds of things.

00:49:32.450 --> 00:49:35.740 align:middle line:84%
But also, "Reverend" Jim Groom
was a bit of a prophet of such

00:49:35.740 --> 00:49:37.690 align:middle line:90%
things.

00:49:37.690 --> 00:49:42.730 align:middle line:84%
So what these
systems really allow

00:49:42.730 --> 00:49:45.690 align:middle line:84%
is a certain kind
of coordination.

00:49:45.690 --> 00:49:48.900 align:middle line:84%
It becomes very,
very easy to say,

00:49:48.900 --> 00:49:51.247 align:middle line:84%
all the faculty are
going to have a page.

00:49:51.247 --> 00:49:53.080 align:middle line:84%
You're all going to
create at the same time.

00:49:53.080 --> 00:49:54.450 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
automatically generated

00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:56.110 align:middle line:84%
for you at the beginning
of the semester.

00:49:56.110 --> 00:49:57.430 align:middle line:84%
Even if you don't
like computers,

00:49:57.430 --> 00:49:59.347 align:middle line:84%
we're still going to be
able to make you do it

00:49:59.347 --> 00:50:01.020 align:middle line:90%
because it's not that hard.

00:50:01.020 --> 00:50:03.510 align:middle line:84%
But there are a couple
of characteristics of it

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:05.890 align:middle line:90%
that I think are not so good.

00:50:05.890 --> 00:50:11.130 align:middle line:84%
I've talked mostly about
the stamped piece of it.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:16.410 align:middle line:84%
There's a way in which the
control of all of the learning

00:50:16.410 --> 00:50:20.580 align:middle line:84%
artifacts is with the
institution and not the people.

00:50:20.580 --> 00:50:23.940 align:middle line:84%
So imagine, imagine
an infrastructure

00:50:23.940 --> 00:50:27.250 align:middle line:84%
for online learning
and higher education

00:50:27.250 --> 00:50:31.280 align:middle line:84%
where you didn't put all the
stuff on someone else's web

00:50:31.280 --> 00:50:31.780 align:middle line:90%
page.

00:50:31.780 --> 00:50:33.307 align:middle line:90%
You put it on your own web page.

00:50:33.307 --> 00:50:34.890 align:middle line:84%
What if, when you
went to do homework,

00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:37.120 align:middle line:84%
you went to do writing,
you went to ask questions,

00:50:37.120 --> 00:50:39.540 align:middle line:84%
it didn't show up on the
school's Canvas site, which

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:41.700 align:middle line:84%
you don't care about,
but it was on your site

00:50:41.700 --> 00:50:42.587 align:middle line:90%
in some kind of way?

00:50:42.587 --> 00:50:44.420 align:middle line:84%
It was in some of space
that you controlled.

00:50:44.420 --> 00:50:47.080 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that was always
craziest to me about these sites

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:49.720 align:middle line:84%
is that we delete
them every 18 months.

00:50:49.720 --> 00:50:51.880 align:middle line:84%
It's a strange thing
to go to students

00:50:51.880 --> 00:50:56.590 align:middle line:84%
and be like, please pour in
your best, most thoughtful, most

00:50:56.590 --> 00:50:59.097 align:middle line:90%
interesting words, ideas.

00:50:59.097 --> 00:51:00.680 align:middle line:84%
Oh, and by the way,
9 months from now,

00:51:00.680 --> 00:51:02.560 align:middle line:84%
they're going to be
automatically deleted.

00:51:02.560 --> 00:51:07.060 align:middle line:84%
That's a strange feature
to me of an online learning

00:51:07.060 --> 00:51:07.820 align:middle line:90%
environment.

00:51:07.820 --> 00:51:10.580 align:middle line:90%


00:51:10.580 --> 00:51:12.718 align:middle line:84%
So me and other
folks for a while

00:51:12.718 --> 00:51:14.260 align:middle line:84%
were really interested
in, well, what

00:51:14.260 --> 00:51:16.660 align:middle line:84%
would an alternative
to this look like?

00:51:16.660 --> 00:51:21.070 align:middle line:84%
What would be something that
was not Blackboard, that was not

00:51:21.070 --> 00:51:26.290 align:middle line:84%
Canvas, that still could have
coordination, but it would have

00:51:26.290 --> 00:51:29.798 align:middle line:84%
some of these characteristics
that the Web 2.0 open web

00:51:29.798 --> 00:51:30.340 align:middle line:90%
movement had?

00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:33.160 align:middle line:84%
Like, it let students
own their own stuff.

00:51:33.160 --> 00:51:36.680 align:middle line:84%
It helped them develop some
technology proficiency.

00:51:36.680 --> 00:51:40.490 align:middle line:84%
It connected people to each
other and to their work.

00:51:40.490 --> 00:51:42.910 align:middle line:84%
It didn't just
hoover up your stuff,

00:51:42.910 --> 00:51:44.950 align:middle line:84%
churn it through, grade
it, and delete it,

00:51:44.950 --> 00:51:47.283 align:middle line:84%
but it would be something
that you owned and cared about

00:51:47.283 --> 00:51:47.820 align:middle line:90%
for a while.

00:51:47.820 --> 00:51:49.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What is in your hands?

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:51.165 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Those are
ice cream sandwiches.

00:51:51.165 --> 00:51:53.490 align:middle line:90%


00:51:53.490 --> 00:51:54.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good.

00:51:54.610 --> 00:51:55.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:51:55.110 --> 00:51:58.290 align:middle line:84%
So it's a good--
it's a good picture.

00:51:58.290 --> 00:52:02.290 align:middle line:84%
It definitely evokes some
of these kinds of pictures.

00:52:02.290 --> 00:52:06.060 align:middle line:84%
Some of these kinds of pictures
ended up on professor Twitter

00:52:06.060 --> 00:52:08.160 align:middle line:90%
handles in 2013.

00:52:08.160 --> 00:52:09.520 align:middle line:90%
I didn't make that picture.

00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:12.250 align:middle line:84%
I had one semester
where, I don't know,

00:52:12.250 --> 00:52:14.500 align:middle line:84%
some students were
enjoying class,

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:16.050 align:middle line:84%
and they sent me
a whole bunch of--

00:52:16.050 --> 00:52:16.690 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:52:16.690 --> 00:52:20.100 align:middle line:84%
How quickly can I
find these things?

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:21.680 align:middle line:90%
There's one of me.

00:52:21.680 --> 00:52:31.740 align:middle line:90%


00:52:31.740 --> 00:52:33.245 align:middle line:90%
Are these going to be on here?

00:52:33.245 --> 00:52:36.660 align:middle line:90%


00:52:36.660 --> 00:52:38.100 align:middle line:90%
There's a few.

00:52:38.100 --> 00:52:39.080 align:middle line:90%
There's this one.

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:43.070 align:middle line:90%


00:52:43.070 --> 00:52:45.140 align:middle line:90%
This one.

00:52:45.140 --> 00:52:48.350 align:middle line:90%
Oh, no, that's a real one.

00:52:48.350 --> 00:52:50.030 align:middle line:90%
Justin is LeBron James.

00:52:50.030 --> 00:52:52.640 align:middle line:84%
A little dicey to be
doing this one these days.

00:52:52.640 --> 00:52:56.090 align:middle line:84%
I probably wouldn't-- you
probably wouldn't put white

00:52:56.090 --> 00:52:58.050 align:middle line:84%
Justin into a
black body anymore.

00:52:58.050 --> 00:53:00.500 align:middle line:90%
But back in the day.

00:53:00.500 --> 00:53:01.220 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:53:01.220 --> 00:53:02.202 align:middle line:90%
For one day for class--

00:53:02.202 --> 00:53:04.410 align:middle line:84%
I think something terrible
had happened in the world,

00:53:04.410 --> 00:53:06.702 align:middle line:84%
and I made them ice cream
sandwiches one day for class.

00:53:06.702 --> 00:53:09.560 align:middle line:84%
And so they made
that picture there.

00:53:09.560 --> 00:53:12.497 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What were
you originally holding?

00:53:12.497 --> 00:53:13.580 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: It was for--

00:53:13.580 --> 00:53:18.890 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] We're getting
to the real issues now.

00:53:18.890 --> 00:53:22.400 align:middle line:84%
I think that I went
to the International

00:53:22.400 --> 00:53:25.140 align:middle line:84%
Society for Technology
in Education Conference,

00:53:25.140 --> 00:53:29.000 align:middle line:84%
and they basically grabbed
a bunch of random people

00:53:29.000 --> 00:53:32.390 align:middle line:84%
and took them to a photo shoot
to generate their own stock

00:53:32.390 --> 00:53:34.560 align:middle line:90%
photos.

00:53:34.560 --> 00:53:36.560 align:middle line:84%
They wanted to have a
whole set of stock photos

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:39.730 align:middle line:84%
that were from the membership
of this organization.

00:53:39.730 --> 00:53:41.170 align:middle line:90%
And so it was just like--

00:53:41.170 --> 00:53:42.712 align:middle line:84%
I don't think I was
holding anything.

00:53:42.712 --> 00:53:46.350 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you can imagine
this as generic invitation

00:53:46.350 --> 00:53:48.450 align:middle line:90%
to some kind of thing.

00:53:48.450 --> 00:53:49.950 align:middle line:84%
But that's where
all that came from.

00:53:49.950 --> 00:53:54.010 align:middle line:90%


00:53:54.010 --> 00:53:55.750 align:middle line:84%
Is this worth your
learning time?

00:53:55.750 --> 00:53:58.200 align:middle line:84%
Hard to say, but
it's pretty fun.

00:53:58.200 --> 00:53:58.700 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:53:58.700 --> 00:54:03.360 align:middle line:84%
This you should
remember a little bit.

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:10.930 align:middle line:84%
This is another branch
of constructionism.

00:54:10.930 --> 00:54:13.000 align:middle line:84%
All these ideas just
repeat themselves.

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:14.880 align:middle line:84%
They get reinvented
over and over again.

00:54:14.880 --> 00:54:19.140 align:middle line:84%
These two Canadian educators,
people who founded the 2007-2008

00:54:19.140 --> 00:54:21.640 align:middle line:84%
original massive
open online courses.

00:54:21.640 --> 00:54:24.510 align:middle line:84%
And they had this idea
called connectivism.

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And they said, part of the
problem with learning theory

00:54:28.470 --> 00:54:32.430 align:middle line:84%
is that it thinks
that learning happens

00:54:32.430 --> 00:54:35.530 align:middle line:84%
and knowledge exists
inside people's heads,

00:54:35.530 --> 00:54:38.870 align:middle line:84%
but learning and knowledge
really exists between people

00:54:38.870 --> 00:54:41.210 align:middle line:90%
more than exists within people.

00:54:41.210 --> 00:54:43.662 align:middle line:84%
There's a famous line,
which you probably all

00:54:43.662 --> 00:54:45.620 align:middle line:84%
would agree with in some
way, that the smartest

00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:49.820 align:middle line:84%
person in the room is the room,
that the collective capacity

00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:53.000 align:middle line:84%
of people is where the heart
of our intelligence lives.

00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:55.325 align:middle line:84%
Connectivism almost
went further and said,

00:54:55.325 --> 00:54:58.280 align:middle line:84%
maybe the only intelligence
in the room is the room.

00:54:58.280 --> 00:55:00.080 align:middle line:84%
Maybe your knowledge,
your understanding

00:55:00.080 --> 00:55:04.430 align:middle line:84%
doesn't exist without social
convention, like language,

00:55:04.430 --> 00:55:07.730 align:middle line:84%
like culture, other kinds
of things like that.

00:55:07.730 --> 00:55:11.990 align:middle line:84%
So this was, at first, an
educational philosophy.

00:55:11.990 --> 00:55:14.450 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it was an
epistemology, maybe

00:55:14.450 --> 00:55:15.840 align:middle line:84%
it was a philosophy
of knowledge.

00:55:15.840 --> 00:55:19.640 align:middle line:84%
But then it got translated
into these pedagogical ideas.

00:55:19.640 --> 00:55:21.690 align:middle line:84%
And the pedagogical
ideas were, say,

00:55:21.690 --> 00:55:26.660 align:middle line:84%
what if you defined courses
where their primary value was

00:55:26.660 --> 00:55:31.850 align:middle line:84%
creating connections
between people and ideas?

00:55:31.850 --> 00:55:33.852 align:middle line:84%
One of the things
Stephen Downes said

00:55:33.852 --> 00:55:35.810 align:middle line:84%
about this, which you'll
find it very connected

00:55:35.810 --> 00:55:37.340 align:middle line:90%
to situated learning.

00:55:37.340 --> 00:55:42.350 align:middle line:84%
He says, if you become a
physicist, what happens?

00:55:42.350 --> 00:55:45.200 align:middle line:84%
Well, you read physics
papers, and you

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:47.660 align:middle line:84%
follow new ideas in
physics, and you figure out

00:55:47.660 --> 00:55:49.430 align:middle line:84%
where physicists
talk to each other

00:55:49.430 --> 00:55:52.580 align:middle line:84%
and where the physics
lounge is and which

00:55:52.580 --> 00:55:54.440 align:middle line:90%
physics conferences to go to--

00:55:54.440 --> 00:55:56.220 align:middle line:84%
I don't know-- what
music physicists like,

00:55:56.220 --> 00:55:57.803 align:middle line:84%
what television shows
they find funny,

00:55:57.803 --> 00:55:59.030 align:middle line:90%
all these kinds of things.

00:55:59.030 --> 00:56:01.580 align:middle line:84%
That is as much a part
of becoming a physicist

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:04.165 align:middle line:84%
as learning formulas and
theories and knowledge

00:56:04.165 --> 00:56:05.040 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:07.400 align:middle line:84%
What if we had educational
systems that took that really

00:56:07.400 --> 00:56:08.130 align:middle line:90%
seriously?

00:56:08.130 --> 00:56:11.840 align:middle line:84%
Like, the job of a
physics class is not just

00:56:11.840 --> 00:56:15.680 align:middle line:84%
to have you be able to learn
some formulas about Newton's

00:56:15.680 --> 00:56:19.140 align:middle line:84%
three laws, but to connect
to other physicists.

00:56:19.140 --> 00:56:22.580 align:middle line:90%
That's kind of a cool idea.

00:56:22.580 --> 00:56:25.020 align:middle line:84%
One of the most radical things
Stephen Downes ever said,

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:27.240 align:middle line:84%
he said something along
the lines of, in my class,

00:56:27.240 --> 00:56:29.480 align:middle line:90%
the content is MacGuffin.

00:56:29.480 --> 00:56:32.210 align:middle line:84%
A MacGuffin is the
thing in a movie

00:56:32.210 --> 00:56:35.430 align:middle line:84%
that's not really important,
that drives the plot forward.

00:56:35.430 --> 00:56:38.055 align:middle line:84%
It's the briefcase that gets
switched in the beginning, which

00:56:38.055 --> 00:56:39.930 align:middle line:84%
helps two people fall
in love with each other

00:56:39.930 --> 00:56:41.220 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:56:41.220 --> 00:56:43.290 align:middle line:84%
The MacGuffin is the
thing that you're chasing

00:56:43.290 --> 00:56:44.707 align:middle line:90%
to make the plot move forward.

00:56:44.707 --> 00:56:46.540 align:middle line:84%
This is a radical
statement for an educator.

00:56:46.540 --> 00:56:48.190 align:middle line:90%
The content is a MacGuffin.

00:56:48.190 --> 00:56:50.980 align:middle line:84%
The thing that I told you the
class is about is a trick.

00:56:50.980 --> 00:56:52.770 align:middle line:84%
The real part of
the class is getting

00:56:52.770 --> 00:56:54.988 align:middle line:84%
you connected to
each other, to things

00:56:54.988 --> 00:56:57.280 align:middle line:84%
that you're interested in,
to other kinds of expertise.

00:56:57.280 --> 00:56:59.250 align:middle line:84%
It's a very radical
set of ideas,

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:03.480 align:middle line:84%
and there are a lot of people
who are profoundly influenced

00:57:03.480 --> 00:57:06.670 align:middle line:84%
by them and people trying
to say, yeah, well,

00:57:06.670 --> 00:57:10.020 align:middle line:84%
what could some
genuine alternative

00:57:10.020 --> 00:57:14.610 align:middle line:84%
architecture to Canvas
that embraced some

00:57:14.610 --> 00:57:19.030 align:middle line:84%
of these ideas of the open web
of Web 2.0 of connectivism,

00:57:19.030 --> 00:57:20.280 align:middle line:90%
what might it look like?

00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:23.170 align:middle line:90%
And so this no longer exists.

00:57:23.170 --> 00:57:26.700 align:middle line:84%
I don't think either of
those links are live anymore.

00:57:26.700 --> 00:57:29.610 align:middle line:84%
But this is what the
website for this class,

00:57:29.610 --> 00:57:33.370 align:middle line:84%
or an earlier version of this
class, used to look like.

00:57:33.370 --> 00:57:37.300 align:middle line:84%
And here's how the
infrastructure of it worked.

00:57:37.300 --> 00:57:40.480 align:middle line:84%
At the beginning of
class, folks were

00:57:40.480 --> 00:57:43.210 align:middle line:84%
assigned to create
spaces where they would

00:57:43.210 --> 00:57:45.123 align:middle line:90%
do the work of this class.

00:57:45.123 --> 00:57:46.790 align:middle line:84%
So you wouldn't have
a learning journal.

00:57:46.790 --> 00:57:50.110 align:middle line:84%
You wouldn't have a Canvas
discussion page or something

00:57:50.110 --> 00:57:50.810 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:57:50.810 --> 00:57:52.220 align:middle line:90%
Go make your own blog.

00:57:52.220 --> 00:57:53.690 align:middle line:90%
Go make your own Tumblr page.

00:57:53.690 --> 00:57:55.070 align:middle line:90%
Go make your own Twitter feed.

00:57:55.070 --> 00:57:56.570 align:middle line:90%
Go make your own Flickr page.

00:57:56.570 --> 00:57:58.750 align:middle line:84%
Make all kinds of
other things like that

00:57:58.750 --> 00:58:03.860 align:middle line:84%
and do all of the work for
this class in your own space.

00:58:03.860 --> 00:58:06.160 align:middle line:84%
Now, one problem with
that is that nobody can

00:58:06.160 --> 00:58:07.570 align:middle line:90%
find that stuff anymore now.

00:58:07.570 --> 00:58:09.170 align:middle line:84%
It's all on other
people's things.

00:58:09.170 --> 00:58:11.770 align:middle line:84%
But that's a solvable
problem, because we're

00:58:11.770 --> 00:58:14.290 align:middle line:84%
going to build this thing
called the Syndication bus.

00:58:14.290 --> 00:58:18.170 align:middle line:84%
And the Syndication Bus is going
to depend on this technology,

00:58:18.170 --> 00:58:18.760 align:middle line:90%
which--

00:58:18.760 --> 00:58:21.177 align:middle line:84%
how many of you are familiar
with real simple syndication?

00:58:21.177 --> 00:58:23.390 align:middle line:84%
Is it something that
you vaguely know exists?

00:58:23.390 --> 00:58:26.470 align:middle line:84%
So again, 10 or
15 years ago, this

00:58:26.470 --> 00:58:29.137 align:middle line:84%
was considered an
essential part of the web.

00:58:29.137 --> 00:58:31.220 align:middle line:84%
And there are many people
who would have told you,

00:58:31.220 --> 00:58:35.140 align:middle line:84%
this is the future of how
people will find things.

00:58:35.140 --> 00:58:41.070 align:middle line:84%
So Jaren was telling us before
about the YouTube algorithm

00:58:41.070 --> 00:58:43.470 align:middle line:84%
kind of controlling what he
watches and what he finds

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:44.350 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:58:44.350 --> 00:58:48.420 align:middle line:84%
An alternative to that was you
subscribe to a series of feeds.

00:58:48.420 --> 00:58:50.940 align:middle line:84%
So you would find all the
bloggers that you like

00:58:50.940 --> 00:58:53.315 align:middle line:84%
or all the video bloggers that
you like or whatever else.

00:58:53.315 --> 00:58:55.740 align:middle line:84%
You would choose their feeds,
and then you would have--

00:58:55.740 --> 00:58:58.590 align:middle line:84%
one really popular technology
was called Google Reader, which

00:58:58.590 --> 00:59:01.630 align:middle line:84%
just gave you the things
that came out of those feeds.

00:59:01.630 --> 00:59:06.120 align:middle line:84%
So instead of being bounced
around by an algorithm

00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:08.370 align:middle line:84%
to things that people think
are popular or interesting

00:59:08.370 --> 00:59:11.500 align:middle line:84%
and things like that, you
have more control over that.

00:59:11.500 --> 00:59:14.820 align:middle line:84%
So basically what
we did was, everyone

00:59:14.820 --> 00:59:16.690 align:middle line:84%
would create their
own online space,

00:59:16.690 --> 00:59:18.930 align:middle line:84%
and then we would use
this Syndication Bus

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:20.320 align:middle line:90%
to aggregate all those things.

00:59:20.320 --> 00:59:22.996 align:middle line:90%
So effectively we would--

00:59:22.996 --> 00:59:26.250 align:middle line:90%
oh, where am I?

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:28.230 align:middle line:84%
There's an animation
there that's missing.

00:59:28.230 --> 00:59:30.840 align:middle line:84%
We would make a copy of
each of these pieces,

00:59:30.840 --> 00:59:33.810 align:middle line:84%
and we would pull the copy
into the Syndication Bus.

00:59:33.810 --> 00:59:35.810 align:middle line:84%
So you owned your
own thing, but we

00:59:35.810 --> 00:59:39.080 align:middle line:84%
would use RSS to grab
one copy of those things,

00:59:39.080 --> 00:59:43.580 align:middle line:84%
and then we would aggregate
them in different kinds of ways.

00:59:43.580 --> 00:59:48.200 align:middle line:84%
So imagine if every time you
wrote in your learning journal--

00:59:48.200 --> 00:59:50.720 align:middle line:84%
you didn't just write in
your own Google Slides,

00:59:50.720 --> 00:59:52.550 align:middle line:90%
but you wrote on a blog--

00:59:52.550 --> 00:59:55.140 align:middle line:84%
anybody could
subscribe to that blog.

00:59:55.140 --> 00:59:57.080 align:middle line:84%
But the course
subscribed to the blog

00:59:57.080 --> 01:00:01.730 align:middle line:84%
and hoovered all of those
things up into one space.

01:00:01.730 --> 01:00:06.170 align:middle line:84%
You could also teach people
other ways of aggregating

01:00:06.170 --> 01:00:08.870 align:middle line:84%
things, like, any time
in any of the spaces

01:00:08.870 --> 01:00:11.060 align:middle line:84%
that you're working,
you share something

01:00:11.060 --> 01:00:14.990 align:middle line:84%
that we might be interested,
tag it with T509 Massive.

01:00:14.990 --> 01:00:18.580 align:middle line:84%
And then what gets populated
in this Syndication Bus Twitter

01:00:18.580 --> 01:00:20.330 align:middle line:84%
channel is all the
tweets that people have

01:00:20.330 --> 01:00:21.910 align:middle line:90%
that are about T509 Massive.

01:00:21.910 --> 01:00:25.700 align:middle line:90%


01:00:25.700 --> 01:00:31.940 align:middle line:84%
Then, when you showed up on the
home page, I as the instructor

01:00:31.940 --> 01:00:33.500 align:middle line:84%
would pick a few
of these things.

01:00:33.500 --> 01:00:35.770 align:middle line:84%
So there was a section
called the Spotlight, where

01:00:35.770 --> 01:00:38.270 align:middle line:84%
I would go through all the stuff
that people were generating

01:00:38.270 --> 01:00:41.050 align:middle line:84%
every week and pick a few things
that I thought were particularly

01:00:41.050 --> 01:00:43.092 align:middle line:84%
interesting, particularly
important, particularly

01:00:43.092 --> 01:00:48.130 align:middle line:84%
funny, stuff like that, and show
them to people to feature them.

01:00:48.130 --> 01:00:52.450 align:middle line:84%
And then-- that's probably
kind of less important.

01:00:52.450 --> 01:00:54.880 align:middle line:84%
But then there
are other spaces--

01:00:54.880 --> 01:00:57.890 align:middle line:84%
I could create spaces that were
about a particular assignment.

01:00:57.890 --> 01:01:00.593 align:middle line:84%
You could say, OK, everybody
is working on your--

01:01:00.593 --> 01:01:02.510 align:middle line:84%
oh, I want to tell you
how I graded the class.

01:01:02.510 --> 01:01:04.750 align:middle line:84%
But I could say, everybody
create your rubric,

01:01:04.750 --> 01:01:06.760 align:middle line:84%
and then pull all
of those things

01:01:06.760 --> 01:01:08.697 align:middle line:84%
together, have a
kind of rubric hub

01:01:08.697 --> 01:01:10.280 align:middle line:84%
that everybody could
see those things.

01:01:10.280 --> 01:01:12.780 align:middle line:84%
And then we had a space, I think
it was called the Fire Hose

01:01:12.780 --> 01:01:14.440 align:middle line:84%
or something like
that, where it was

01:01:14.440 --> 01:01:17.350 align:middle line:90%
everything that was coming in.

01:01:17.350 --> 01:01:19.030 align:middle line:84%
There were probably
50 or 60 people

01:01:19.030 --> 01:01:20.540 align:middle line:84%
who took this
version of the class,

01:01:20.540 --> 01:01:22.123 align:middle line:84%
and so there was no
way that everybody

01:01:22.123 --> 01:01:24.380 align:middle line:84%
was going to read everything
that everyone else did.

01:01:24.380 --> 01:01:28.550 align:middle line:84%
But a part of class every
week was finding out,

01:01:28.550 --> 01:01:30.557 align:middle line:90%
who are you connected with?

01:01:30.557 --> 01:01:31.890 align:middle line:90%
Who are you finding interesting?

01:01:31.890 --> 01:01:33.180 align:middle line:90%
Who are you learning from?

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:35.180 align:middle line:84%
Oh, you four were
English teachers

01:01:35.180 --> 01:01:36.430 align:middle line:90%
before you came in this class?

01:01:36.430 --> 01:01:38.097 align:middle line:84%
You should be networked
with each other.

01:01:38.097 --> 01:01:39.530 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, you
should be networked

01:01:39.530 --> 01:01:41.150 align:middle line:90%
to other English teachers.

01:01:41.150 --> 01:01:42.600 align:middle line:84%
The things that
you're generating,

01:01:42.600 --> 01:01:44.910 align:middle line:84%
if you're putting a bunch
of work into this class,

01:01:44.910 --> 01:01:47.737 align:middle line:84%
why just have it sit
in a discussion forum

01:01:47.737 --> 01:01:49.320 align:middle line:84%
that no one's ever
going to see again.

01:01:49.320 --> 01:01:51.560 align:middle line:84%
Put it in this space where you
can share with other people

01:01:51.560 --> 01:01:52.393 align:middle line:90%
and stuff like that.

01:01:52.393 --> 01:01:59.060 align:middle line:90%


01:01:59.060 --> 01:02:04.040 align:middle line:84%
If you take this idea
of letting people

01:02:04.040 --> 01:02:07.430 align:middle line:84%
choose how they want to share,
who they want to connect

01:02:07.430 --> 01:02:11.585 align:middle line:84%
with seriously, the more and
more you get to this idea of,

01:02:11.585 --> 01:02:13.692 align:middle line:90%
there's no right way to do this.

01:02:13.692 --> 01:02:15.900 align:middle line:84%
You're all coming to this
class with different goals.

01:02:15.900 --> 01:02:19.070 align:middle line:84%
Maybe some of you really want
to connect with other edtech

01:02:19.070 --> 01:02:21.650 align:middle line:84%
companies and are interested
in being a founder

01:02:21.650 --> 01:02:22.710 align:middle line:90%
or working for a startup.

01:02:22.710 --> 01:02:24.630 align:middle line:84%
Maybe some of you want to
go into education research.

01:02:24.630 --> 01:02:26.010 align:middle line:84%
Maybe some of you want
to go into teaching.

01:02:26.010 --> 01:02:27.910 align:middle line:84%
Why should you all
have the same criteria

01:02:27.910 --> 01:02:30.118 align:middle line:84%
for what you're writing
about or what you're sharing?

01:02:30.118 --> 01:02:31.692 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you just
tell me what it is

01:02:31.692 --> 01:02:32.900 align:middle line:90%
that you want to learn about?

01:02:32.900 --> 01:02:34.608 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you tell me
what your goals are

01:02:34.608 --> 01:02:36.970 align:middle line:90%
for this kind of communication?

01:02:36.970 --> 01:02:41.313 align:middle line:84%
And so people would create
their own rubrics about--

01:02:41.313 --> 01:02:43.730 align:middle line:84%
essentially, you tell me what
your blog is supposed to do,

01:02:43.730 --> 01:02:44.813 align:middle line:90%
is supposed to accomplish.

01:02:44.813 --> 01:02:46.990 align:middle line:84%
I think at one point,
there was a part

01:02:46.990 --> 01:02:49.332 align:middle line:84%
of the assignment which
you had to say, describe

01:02:49.332 --> 01:02:51.790 align:middle line:84%
how your writing is going to
benefit you and your learning,

01:02:51.790 --> 01:02:53.110 align:middle line:84%
how your writing
is going to benefit

01:02:53.110 --> 01:02:55.540 align:middle line:84%
the other people in this class,
and how your writing might

01:02:55.540 --> 01:02:57.697 align:middle line:84%
plausibly benefit some
other community somewhere

01:02:57.697 --> 01:02:59.780 align:middle line:84%
else that you're connected
to in some kind of way.

01:02:59.780 --> 01:03:00.560 align:middle line:90%
This was a professional--

01:03:00.560 --> 01:03:02.600 align:middle line:84%
I taught this at the Harvard
Graduate School of Education.

01:03:02.600 --> 01:03:04.017 align:middle line:84%
So people were,
for the most part,

01:03:04.017 --> 01:03:06.880 align:middle line:84%
already coming in with some
kind of professional communities

01:03:06.880 --> 01:03:10.300 align:middle line:90%
from outside to network.

01:03:10.300 --> 01:03:16.810 align:middle line:84%
And there were lots
of other people

01:03:16.810 --> 01:03:19.540 align:middle line:84%
who were doing
this kind of thing.

01:03:19.540 --> 01:03:21.640 align:middle line:84%
This was a course called
Connected Courses, which

01:03:21.640 --> 01:03:24.960 align:middle line:84%
was basically teaching people
across higher education how

01:03:24.960 --> 01:03:27.270 align:middle line:84%
you would go about
creating these courses.

01:03:27.270 --> 01:03:29.755 align:middle line:84%
But people built
them in anthropology.

01:03:29.755 --> 01:03:32.130 align:middle line:84%
Probably one of the most famous
ones, which I think still

01:03:32.130 --> 01:03:36.090 align:middle line:84%
has a bunch of stuff which
exists, is called DS106, Digital

01:03:36.090 --> 01:03:39.660 align:middle line:84%
Storytelling 106, which
was a course offered

01:03:39.660 --> 01:03:41.410 align:middle line:84%
at the University
of Mary Washington,

01:03:41.410 --> 01:03:44.340 align:middle line:84%
but essentially syndicated to
a bunch of other universities

01:03:44.340 --> 01:03:46.170 align:middle line:90%
and also built in such a way--

01:03:46.170 --> 01:03:49.710 align:middle line:84%
you can probably still go
to ds106.org right now.

01:03:49.710 --> 01:03:53.370 align:middle line:84%
And it was mostly about
creative assignments.

01:03:53.370 --> 01:03:57.653 align:middle line:84%
They had a feature called
the Daily Create where,

01:03:57.653 --> 01:03:59.320 align:middle line:84%
if you took this class
four days a week,

01:03:59.320 --> 01:04:00.945 align:middle line:84%
you were supposed to
do a daily create.

01:04:00.945 --> 01:04:03.870 align:middle line:84%
It might be something
like-- my favorite one was,

01:04:03.870 --> 01:04:06.330 align:middle line:84%
pull out your keychain
and make a short video

01:04:06.330 --> 01:04:09.180 align:middle line:84%
where you tell the
story of your keychain.

01:04:09.180 --> 01:04:10.750 align:middle line:90%
Where does each key come from?

01:04:10.750 --> 01:04:11.830 align:middle line:90%
What does it mean to you?

01:04:11.830 --> 01:04:14.770 align:middle line:90%
Other kinds of things like that.

01:04:14.770 --> 01:04:19.440 align:middle line:84%
Figuring out how to make
their way across the web.

01:04:19.440 --> 01:04:22.510 align:middle line:84%
So I told you this was a
story, essentially, of a failed

01:04:22.510 --> 01:04:24.890 align:middle line:90%
enterprise.

01:04:24.890 --> 01:04:26.980 align:middle line:84%
Clearly, I'm not teaching
like this anymore.

01:04:26.980 --> 01:04:29.150 align:middle line:84%
You won't find
many other classes.

01:04:29.150 --> 01:04:32.390 align:middle line:84%
You'll find the idea
is kind out there,

01:04:32.390 --> 01:04:37.210 align:middle line:84%
percolating in different
spaces, but probably

01:04:37.210 --> 01:04:38.680 align:middle line:90%
doesn't really exist.

01:04:38.680 --> 01:04:39.680 align:middle line:90%
Why did it fail?

01:04:39.680 --> 01:04:41.180 align:middle line:90%
Probably two big reasons.

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:43.000 align:middle line:84%
One that we'll keep
coming back to,

01:04:43.000 --> 01:04:45.610 align:middle line:84%
which I've termed the Curse
of the Familiar, which is,

01:04:45.610 --> 01:04:47.170 align:middle line:90%
it's just weird.

01:04:47.170 --> 01:04:48.350 align:middle line:90%
It's different.

01:04:48.350 --> 01:04:49.520 align:middle line:90%
It's unusual.

01:04:49.520 --> 01:04:51.140 align:middle line:84%
It takes a bunch
of time to learn.

01:04:51.140 --> 01:04:52.600 align:middle line:84%
On the one hand,
I think people who

01:04:52.600 --> 01:04:54.410 align:middle line:84%
participate in these
classes were like,

01:04:54.410 --> 01:04:56.410 align:middle line:84%
oh man, I learned a lot
of stuff about blogging,

01:04:56.410 --> 01:04:58.743 align:middle line:84%
about using Twitter, about
connecting with other people,

01:04:58.743 --> 01:04:59.750 align:middle line:90%
about social networking.

01:04:59.750 --> 01:05:04.245 align:middle line:84%
But, my guy, the class was
intermediate statistics.

01:05:04.245 --> 01:05:05.620 align:middle line:84%
That's not what
you were supposed

01:05:05.620 --> 01:05:07.210 align:middle line:90%
to be learning in this thing.

01:05:07.210 --> 01:05:09.400 align:middle line:84%
There were a handful
of universities

01:05:09.400 --> 01:05:12.280 align:middle line:84%
that built a collaborative
infrastructure

01:05:12.280 --> 01:05:15.850 align:middle line:84%
around this stuff, in the
hopes that you could drive down

01:05:15.850 --> 01:05:16.880 align:middle line:90%
some of the complexity.

01:05:16.880 --> 01:05:20.710 align:middle line:84%
So this guy, Jim Groom, built a
company called Reclaim Hosting,

01:05:20.710 --> 01:05:22.350 align:middle line:90%
which tried to make it really--

01:05:22.350 --> 01:05:24.750 align:middle line:84%
universities could
sign up to make

01:05:24.750 --> 01:05:27.467 align:middle line:84%
it really easy for every student
in the school to start a blog.

01:05:27.467 --> 01:05:29.800 align:middle line:84%
And then you teach them all
about blogging a little bit,

01:05:29.800 --> 01:05:32.133 align:middle line:84%
and then maybe you could get
away from discussion forums

01:05:32.133 --> 01:05:35.125 align:middle line:84%
and have people do most of their
short form writing on blogs.

01:05:35.125 --> 01:05:37.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there are some people out
there that are still trying it,

01:05:37.750 --> 01:05:40.800 align:middle line:90%
but not that much.

01:05:40.800 --> 01:05:46.500 align:middle line:84%
And then another huge
challenge with asking people

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:49.800 align:middle line:84%
to write publicly is
that we are not all

01:05:49.800 --> 01:05:52.620 align:middle line:84%
equally vulnerable
to the consequences

01:05:52.620 --> 01:05:55.180 align:middle line:90%
of having our writing be read.

01:05:55.180 --> 01:05:57.660 align:middle line:90%
Anybody recognize this person?

01:05:57.660 --> 01:06:02.160 align:middle line:84%
She's a woman named
Anita Sarkeesian, who

01:06:02.160 --> 01:06:06.030 align:middle line:84%
had a website called
Feminist Frequency, which

01:06:06.030 --> 01:06:10.440 align:middle line:84%
was one of the initial
spaces that was related

01:06:10.440 --> 01:06:13.260 align:middle line:90%
to what became Gamergate.

01:06:13.260 --> 01:06:16.620 align:middle line:84%
She made a bunch of
videos basically about

01:06:16.620 --> 01:06:19.300 align:middle line:84%
anti-feminist themes
in video games,

01:06:19.300 --> 01:06:25.315 align:middle line:84%
like how there's always a
princess that needs to be saved,

01:06:25.315 --> 01:06:26.690 align:middle line:84%
and the princess
doesn't actually

01:06:26.690 --> 01:06:28.670 align:middle line:84%
have to-- doesn't get to
have her own character

01:06:28.670 --> 01:06:30.240 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:37.820 align:middle line:84%
And just piled on ruthlessly by
all kinds of people, especially

01:06:37.820 --> 01:06:40.580 align:middle line:84%
people who saw gaming
as a hobby, who didn't

01:06:40.580 --> 01:06:42.710 align:middle line:90%
want these tropes changed.

01:06:42.710 --> 01:06:45.768 align:middle line:84%
And so people get
doxxed and swatted

01:06:45.768 --> 01:06:48.060 align:middle line:84%
and all kinds of-- are you
all familiar with Gamergate?

01:06:48.060 --> 01:06:49.070 align:middle line:90%
Is that a phrase that--

01:06:49.070 --> 01:06:49.850 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:06:49.850 --> 01:06:53.630 align:middle line:84%
So I don't even remember
what year this was now.

01:06:53.630 --> 01:06:54.380 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:06:54.380 --> 01:06:55.600 align:middle line:84%
In the Media Studies
department, we

01:06:55.600 --> 01:06:57.433 align:middle line:84%
should be teaching a
class called Gamergate.

01:06:57.433 --> 01:06:58.790 align:middle line:90%
It was kind of--

01:06:58.790 --> 01:07:02.510 align:middle line:84%
it seemed ludicrous
at the time, but it

01:07:02.510 --> 01:07:08.960 align:middle line:84%
was a way of organizing
groups of people

01:07:08.960 --> 01:07:11.360 align:middle line:84%
to effectively
suppress communication

01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:14.700 align:middle line:84%
from subaltern
perspectives, from people

01:07:14.700 --> 01:07:16.700 align:middle line:84%
who are trying to say
different kinds of things.

01:07:16.700 --> 01:07:20.440 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, for me as a
pretty privileged white dude

01:07:20.440 --> 01:07:22.060 align:middle line:84%
blogging my way
through the world,

01:07:22.060 --> 01:07:24.470 align:middle line:84%
it's relatively safe and
relatively straightforward.

01:07:24.470 --> 01:07:26.303 align:middle line:84%
But if I have 50 or 60
students in my class,

01:07:26.303 --> 01:07:28.762 align:middle line:84%
they're all going to be coming
from different perspectives,

01:07:28.762 --> 01:07:30.430 align:middle line:84%
and they're all going
to be inequitably

01:07:30.430 --> 01:07:33.070 align:middle line:84%
vulnerable to writing
something which

01:07:33.070 --> 01:07:36.500 align:middle line:84%
catches the attention in
negative ways of big crowds,

01:07:36.500 --> 01:07:39.268 align:middle line:84%
or offends people, or
messes up their relationship

01:07:39.268 --> 01:07:40.310 align:middle line:90%
or other kinds of things.

01:07:40.310 --> 01:07:43.480 align:middle line:84%
I mean, part of what you want
to do in writing in a class

01:07:43.480 --> 01:07:45.515 align:middle line:84%
is let people be
experimental and try

01:07:45.515 --> 01:07:47.140 align:middle line:84%
on ideas that they
don't agree with yet

01:07:47.140 --> 01:07:48.140 align:middle line:90%
or don't understand yet.

01:07:48.140 --> 01:07:52.150 align:middle line:90%


01:07:52.150 --> 01:07:54.650 align:middle line:84%
And then, when I was
doing this in 2013,

01:07:54.650 --> 01:07:57.200 align:middle line:84%
that felt a little
bit risky, maybe.

01:07:57.200 --> 01:08:00.730 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I could imagine someone
might take down a post that they

01:08:00.730 --> 01:08:04.420 align:middle line:84%
decide wasn't well worded or
disagree with, things like that.

01:08:04.420 --> 01:08:09.020 align:middle line:84%
In 2024, it seems
substantially more risky.

01:08:09.020 --> 01:08:14.860 align:middle line:84%
I would say not just because
of heightened sensitivities,

01:08:14.860 --> 01:08:19.100 align:middle line:84%
but because actors in
society have learned

01:08:19.100 --> 01:08:25.399 align:middle line:84%
how to broadcast messages
that appear online

01:08:25.399 --> 01:08:28.040 align:middle line:84%
in various kinds of
places and to use them

01:08:28.040 --> 01:08:31.609 align:middle line:84%
to generate outrage and
drive traffic and those kinds

01:08:31.609 --> 01:08:34.189 align:middle line:90%
of things.

01:08:34.189 --> 01:08:37.460 align:middle line:84%
Oh, this was the
company, Reclaim Hosting.

01:08:37.460 --> 01:08:41.330 align:middle line:84%
And somebody should
Google Reclaim Hosting

01:08:41.330 --> 01:08:45.500 align:middle line:84%
and see if they're still
known for education.

01:08:45.500 --> 01:08:47.689 align:middle line:84%
It was a project
that was basically--

01:08:47.689 --> 01:08:51.200 align:middle line:84%
it was trying to take everything
that I just described and figure

01:08:51.200 --> 01:08:55.170 align:middle line:84%
out, how do you make a really
simple front end for it

01:08:55.170 --> 01:08:57.710 align:middle line:84%
so it's not hard
for people to build,

01:08:57.710 --> 01:09:00.240 align:middle line:84%
but it still lets
people own stuff?

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:04.670 align:middle line:84%
It still lets people be the
owner of their own means

01:09:04.670 --> 01:09:07.520 align:middle line:90%
of production.

01:09:07.520 --> 01:09:09.979 align:middle line:84%
But it's something
that I think will--

01:09:09.979 --> 01:09:16.960 align:middle line:84%
by far, the most exciting
part of teaching these courses

01:09:16.960 --> 01:09:20.140 align:middle line:84%
was realizing that
people were still

01:09:20.140 --> 01:09:23.229 align:middle line:84%
using this infrastructure in
various kinds of ways years

01:09:23.229 --> 01:09:24.439 align:middle line:90%
after the course ended.

01:09:24.439 --> 01:09:26.920 align:middle line:84%
So I think I taught
this course in 2013.

01:09:26.920 --> 01:09:30.595 align:middle line:84%
And still, in 2015, people
were posting on their blogs,

01:09:30.595 --> 01:09:31.720 align:middle line:90%
connecting with each other.

01:09:31.720 --> 01:09:33.220 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, the
equivalent of that

01:09:33.220 --> 01:09:37.569 align:middle line:84%
would be, if you're in 18.06 now
and it has a Piazza forum, what

01:09:37.569 --> 01:09:40.270 align:middle line:84%
if, three years from now,
you were inspired to hop back

01:09:40.270 --> 01:09:43.410 align:middle line:84%
in there and still-- everyone's
looking at me like, good god,

01:09:43.410 --> 01:09:44.660 align:middle line:90%
I would never do such a thing.

01:09:44.660 --> 01:09:47.663 align:middle line:84%
But you wouldn't do such a
thing because you don't own it,

01:09:47.663 --> 01:09:50.080 align:middle line:84%
and it's not yours, and you
can't take it in the direction

01:09:50.080 --> 01:09:51.163 align:middle line:90%
that you're interested in.

01:09:51.163 --> 01:09:53.770 align:middle line:84%
But in higher education, if
we gave you an infrastructure

01:09:53.770 --> 01:09:56.350 align:middle line:84%
that you had some
control over, you

01:09:56.350 --> 01:09:57.792 align:middle line:90%
might be substantially more--

01:09:57.792 --> 01:09:59.500 align:middle line:84%
I mean, at least the
students at the time

01:09:59.500 --> 01:10:07.060 align:middle line:84%
were interested in continuing to
pursue these kinds of learning

01:10:07.060 --> 01:10:09.310 align:middle line:90%
experiences.

01:10:09.310 --> 01:10:14.880 align:middle line:84%
But I would say the
degree to which people

01:10:14.880 --> 01:10:19.210 align:middle line:84%
feel much more individual threat
about sharing things online,

01:10:19.210 --> 01:10:22.020 align:middle line:84%
especially in
really open spaces,

01:10:22.020 --> 01:10:25.410 align:middle line:84%
and just the difficulty
of the ramp-up time

01:10:25.410 --> 01:10:26.940 align:middle line:84%
of building all
this infrastructure,

01:10:26.940 --> 01:10:31.620 align:middle line:84%
has made it more or less
an interesting dead end

01:10:31.620 --> 01:10:34.120 align:middle line:84%
and something kind of fun
to learn from and explore.

01:10:34.120 --> 01:10:38.590 align:middle line:84%
It probably also illustrates
more of what you'll read about.

01:10:38.590 --> 01:10:41.670 align:middle line:84%
This is an effort
in higher education

01:10:41.670 --> 01:10:43.890 align:middle line:90%
that basically foundered.

01:10:43.890 --> 01:10:45.780 align:middle line:84%
You'll read about
other kinds of things

01:10:45.780 --> 01:10:49.420 align:middle line:84%
that foundered in
different ways in K-12,

01:10:49.420 --> 01:10:52.210 align:middle line:84%
or the challenge to make them
not founder, for next week.

01:10:52.210 --> 01:10:54.870 align:middle line:84%
There's all this
amazing learning

01:10:54.870 --> 01:10:59.940 align:middle line:84%
that happens in people's
informal learning experiences.

01:10:59.940 --> 01:11:03.600 align:middle line:84%
Why is it so hard to
bring those practices

01:11:03.600 --> 01:11:04.780 align:middle line:90%
into school environments?

01:11:04.780 --> 01:11:08.100 align:middle line:84%
What is the nature of school
environments that makes it

01:11:08.100 --> 01:11:11.970 align:middle line:90%
difficult for that to happen?

01:11:11.970 --> 01:11:12.747 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:11:12.747 --> 01:11:14.330 align:middle line:84%
I think that's where
I got to the end.

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:17.200 align:middle line:84%
What questions do
folks have or what--

01:11:17.200 --> 01:11:22.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: My question is, what
place does online community

01:11:22.750 --> 01:11:29.428 align:middle line:84%
building without the presence
of instructors have in this?

01:11:29.428 --> 01:11:30.220 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

01:11:30.220 --> 01:11:38.270 align:middle line:84%
If this worked because you owned
your own piece of writing--

01:11:38.270 --> 01:11:41.080 align:middle line:84%
I'm teaching this class
at a professional school--

01:11:41.080 --> 01:11:45.430 align:middle line:84%
you might find that actually,
what motivates you most to write

01:11:45.430 --> 01:11:48.100 align:middle line:84%
these assignments
is that you found

01:11:48.100 --> 01:11:51.010 align:middle line:84%
a bunch of other sixth
grade science teachers

01:11:51.010 --> 01:11:52.910 align:middle line:84%
that are reading
your blog posts,

01:11:52.910 --> 01:11:57.800 align:middle line:84%
and you increasingly
care less that Justin,

01:11:57.800 --> 01:12:00.430 align:middle line:84%
the instructor of this
class, is reading your stuff

01:12:00.430 --> 01:12:03.220 align:middle line:84%
and more like, hey, I'm
getting my ideas out there

01:12:03.220 --> 01:12:06.580 align:middle line:84%
and connecting more with
other Earth science teachers.

01:12:06.580 --> 01:12:08.740 align:middle line:84%
And that, I think,
for a lot of-- when

01:12:08.740 --> 01:12:11.940 align:middle line:84%
it was most successful,
that for people

01:12:11.940 --> 01:12:16.780 align:middle line:84%
just felt like work that
was super real and urgent.

01:12:16.780 --> 01:12:18.660 align:middle line:84%
The other thing is,
most of the things--

01:12:18.660 --> 01:12:19.740 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:12:19.740 --> 01:12:25.373 align:middle line:84%
When people got responses,
there wasn't a whole lot of--

01:12:25.373 --> 01:12:26.790 align:middle line:84%
there was probably
some, but there

01:12:26.790 --> 01:12:30.900 align:middle line:84%
wasn't a whole lot of, you are
required to reply to at least

01:12:30.900 --> 01:12:33.630 align:middle line:90%
two people kinds of things.

01:12:33.630 --> 01:12:37.990 align:middle line:84%
There was a lot of, people
would write blog posts,

01:12:37.990 --> 01:12:41.370 align:middle line:84%
but they would write
blog posts that tagged

01:12:41.370 --> 01:12:43.930 align:middle line:84%
or posted back to
someone else's post.

01:12:43.930 --> 01:12:48.150 align:middle line:84%
So it was just kind of like
a natural conversation that

01:12:48.150 --> 01:12:50.040 align:middle line:90%
was happening and unfolding.

01:12:50.040 --> 01:12:52.740 align:middle line:84%
Where it was most
successful, what

01:12:52.740 --> 01:12:54.810 align:middle line:84%
you felt like as
an instructor, is

01:12:54.810 --> 01:12:59.550 align:middle line:84%
you were just putting
some fuel for this fire

01:12:59.550 --> 01:13:00.730 align:middle line:90%
to burn on its own.

01:13:00.730 --> 01:13:03.328 align:middle line:84%
You're just like, here,
check out these things.

01:13:03.328 --> 01:13:05.370 align:middle line:84%
But then people would take
them in the directions

01:13:05.370 --> 01:13:06.940 align:middle line:84%
that they were most interested
in and excited about.

01:13:06.940 --> 01:13:08.232 align:middle line:90%
Does that answer your question?

01:13:08.232 --> 01:13:11.330 align:middle line:90%


01:13:11.330 --> 01:13:13.337 align:middle line:90%
Other thoughts or things that--

01:13:13.337 --> 01:13:15.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How does this
play into the Google

01:13:15.170 --> 01:13:18.200 align:middle line:90%
Doc that runs this course?

01:13:18.200 --> 01:13:19.340 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Huh.

01:13:19.340 --> 01:13:20.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:20.960 --> 01:13:23.480 align:middle line:90%
Well, some of it is--

01:13:23.480 --> 01:13:26.660 align:middle line:84%
probably the main
lingering piece

01:13:26.660 --> 01:13:29.930 align:middle line:84%
is that it's a pain in
the ass to show my Canvas

01:13:29.930 --> 01:13:31.230 align:middle line:90%
site to someone else.

01:13:31.230 --> 01:13:34.190 align:middle line:84%
And one of the main
things that I want to do

01:13:34.190 --> 01:13:38.810 align:middle line:84%
is, I don't know, in my career
of teaching, be able to quickly

01:13:38.810 --> 01:13:41.150 align:middle line:84%
to explain to other
people how I teach.

01:13:41.150 --> 01:13:44.690 align:middle line:84%
So one of the best things about
having the whole course live

01:13:44.690 --> 01:13:47.390 align:middle line:84%
on a Google Doc is
any new professor

01:13:47.390 --> 01:13:49.980 align:middle line:84%
who's just starting teaching
EdTech and Society, it's like,

01:13:49.980 --> 01:13:51.110 align:middle line:90%
here's a whole course.

01:13:51.110 --> 01:13:52.190 align:middle line:84%
Take it and-- I mean,
they're probably

01:13:52.190 --> 01:13:53.900 align:middle line:84%
taking it and
shoving it bit by bit

01:13:53.900 --> 01:13:57.470 align:middle line:84%
into Canvas, which I've done
periodically for some things.

01:13:57.470 --> 01:14:01.647 align:middle line:84%
That any of you who
want to sign up for my--

01:14:01.647 --> 01:14:03.980 align:middle line:84%
future people who are interested
in this class, syllabus

01:14:03.980 --> 01:14:04.850 align:middle line:90%
is there.

01:14:04.850 --> 01:14:06.840 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's even got
the revision history.

01:14:06.840 --> 01:14:08.220 align:middle line:84%
You can not only see--
nobody does that,

01:14:08.220 --> 01:14:09.762 align:middle line:84%
but you can not only
see what we did.

01:14:09.762 --> 01:14:12.520 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I would also say,
earlier versions of that--

01:14:12.520 --> 01:14:17.980 align:middle line:84%
when the web was more active
in this way, over the summer,

01:14:17.980 --> 01:14:19.630 align:middle line:84%
I would post, here
are all the changes

01:14:19.630 --> 01:14:22.588 align:middle line:84%
that I made to my syllabus and
circulate it around the hashtags

01:14:22.588 --> 01:14:24.130 align:middle line:84%
or other networks
of people that were

01:14:24.130 --> 01:14:25.520 align:middle line:90%
active in those conversations.

01:14:25.520 --> 01:14:28.750 align:middle line:84%
And people would definitely
jump into the syllabus

01:14:28.750 --> 01:14:31.532 align:middle line:84%
and add comments and be like,
oh, this new book just came out,

01:14:31.532 --> 01:14:33.740 align:middle line:84%
or these new resources came
out and things like that.

01:14:33.740 --> 01:14:35.980 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it existed
as, for a period

01:14:35.980 --> 01:14:39.190 align:middle line:84%
of time, a much more
social document,

01:14:39.190 --> 01:14:42.708 align:middle line:84%
again, when there were
a lot of educators,

01:14:42.708 --> 01:14:45.250 align:middle line:84%
especially people interested in
online learning and education

01:14:45.250 --> 01:14:47.170 align:middle line:84%
technology, who
were pretty active

01:14:47.170 --> 01:14:49.180 align:middle line:90%
in those kinds of communities.

01:14:49.180 --> 01:14:51.020 align:middle line:84%
And so you could
do stuff like that.

01:14:51.020 --> 01:14:53.890 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's mostly just
a legacy of, editing

01:14:53.890 --> 01:14:56.765 align:middle line:84%
that thing is so much
easier than editing Canvas.

01:14:56.765 --> 01:14:58.640 align:middle line:84%
Anytime I want to change
something in Canvas,

01:14:58.640 --> 01:15:01.130 align:middle line:84%
I have to do it in three
places and click 15 buttons.

01:15:01.130 --> 01:15:03.410 align:middle line:90%
The Google Doc, I just edit.

01:15:03.410 --> 01:15:03.910 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:15:03.910 --> 01:15:06.370 align:middle line:84%
So that was a little bit
in some of these ideas

01:15:06.370 --> 01:15:08.290 align:middle line:84%
of peer-guided learning
in higher education.

01:15:08.290 --> 01:15:10.848 align:middle line:84%
We'll see some more
of them in K-12.

01:15:10.848 --> 01:15:13.140 align:middle line:84%
I think you're going to read
some stuff from Mimi Ito--

01:15:13.140 --> 01:15:19.510 align:middle line:84%
and what else did I make
you do for next time?--

01:15:19.510 --> 01:15:24.370 align:middle line:84%
and Mitch Resnick about
Scratch, which Scratch

01:15:24.370 --> 01:15:29.190 align:middle line:84%
is probably the most successful
peer-guided learning environment

01:15:29.190 --> 01:15:30.968 align:middle line:84%
which has made its
way into K-12 schools.

01:15:30.968 --> 01:15:32.760 align:middle line:84%
Although one of the
things that we'll learn

01:15:32.760 --> 01:15:35.940 align:middle line:84%
is that when it gets
there, it kind of gets

01:15:35.940 --> 01:15:38.940 align:middle line:84%
schoolified in ways that are
horrific to the people who

01:15:38.940 --> 01:15:43.240 align:middle line:84%
made it, but more to follow
when we discuss that on Monday.

01:15:43.240 --> 01:15:43.740 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:15:43.740 --> 01:15:45.900 align:middle line:84%
Have a wonderful rest of
your week and weekend,

01:15:45.900 --> 01:15:48.440 align:middle line:90%
and I will see you next week.

01:15:48.440 --> 01:16:06.000 align:middle line:90%